Petr Cech: Transfer at the last minute?
Anything's possible
Today is the last day when it is still possible to jump through the transfer window and make a big transfer.
There'll be another chance in winter.
Petr Cech, Chelsea's substitute goalkeeper at the moment, does not now sit over a new professional contract in Madrid, Monaco or Paris.
He sits in the Smíchov NH Hotel, speaks with Czech journalists and prepares with the Czech national team for a game general with the Americans and subsequently also for a sharp start qualifying for Euro 2016 against the Netherlands.
In the Chelsea goal, Thibaut Courtoios takes precedence in the new Premier League season, with Cech putting himself in a substitute position for the first time in a decade at the London club.
Some time ago it was speculated that Real Madrid might be interested in the Czech.
At the start of the season, he lost the Spanish Super Cup in clashes with Atletico and did not even manage to enter the league season when he lost 2:4 in San Sebastián.
There has also been speculation about Paris SG's interest.
He made two wins and two draws into the French league season, shooting St. Etienne 5:0 on Sunday.
Relatively, most of them are pushing the AS Monaco club.
He has a 1-1-2 record in the league so far and broke up with Lille 1:1 at the weekend.
On tabloid English websites, reports popped up today that they might want a Cech at Queens Park Rangers.
According to English bookmakers, however, Cech's last-minute departure from Chelsea is not very likely.
Skybet put odds 4:1 on him.
Peter, is it possible that in the remaining few hours you will change the jersey?
I don't count on it, but there's never a hundred percent in football.
Perhaps during the day there will be an offer that cannot be rejected.
What would such an offer have to look like?
(laughs) "Well, I don't know yet what such an offer that can't be rejected actually looks.
It would have to come to an agreement from all parties First of all, it would depend on Chelsea's stance on whether or not to address the club's eventually.
Is it even technically feasible to do such a transfer now that you are in Prague?
Wouldn't it be necessary for you to sign a possible new contract somewhere in your new location?
That's not what I was thinking.
But there are a lot of players in that situation today, they're all somewhere at representative reunions, and I'm sure a lot of transfers are still closed today.
Somehow it would be feasible.
How many clubs are there that could afford you?
Well, I don't really know.
So you're not pushing to leave?
The situation when I didn't catch in the first three games of the new season is new to me after fifteen years of my career.
However, there's no reason to panic, pack up and go somewhere.
I want to continue to participate in that Chelsea goalkeeper's position and it's up to the coach who he chooses, what decisions he's going to make over the next few weeks.
What role does your family play in your decision-making?
I take into account all the factors.
The important thing for me, of course, is the sports aspect, but I also look at it from the personal side, to which the needs of the family of course belongs.
Is it possible that you'd catch for another club in England?
That's a question I thought would never concern me.
But anything is possible in football.
I don't want to rule anything out.
I don't know the answer.
Prime ministers of India and Japan at meeting in Tokyo
India's new Prime Minister Narendra Modi is in Tokyo at a meeting with his Japanese counterpart Shinzo Abe. On his first major visit since May's victory in the election, he is due to discuss economic and security commitments.
Modi is on a five-day trip to Japan to cement economic ties there with the world's third-largest economy.
There are first and foremost plans for more extensive cooperation in the field of nuclear energy.
According to the correspondents, India also hopes that the two nations will agree to cooperate on defense issues.
Police in Karrata arrest 20-year-old after motorcycle hound
The motorbike was halted after driving 125 km/h with a zone with a maximum speed of 70 km/h and drove into the bush to escape police officers.
Patroling traffic police tried to stop a blue motorbike on the side of the road in Karrata in the morning when they noticed that 125 km/h were driving while exiting a gas station on Bathgate Road.
According to police, the driver didn't stop and proceeded after Burgess Road and then turned into the bush, so officers lost sight of him.
The motorbike and the person who matched the driver's description were then spotted at a house in Walcott Way, Bulgara.
Karrath police have accused the 20-year-old of disobeying a call to stop and careless proceedings.
He'll stand trial in Karrata on September 23.
The motorcycle was seized for three months.
George Webster charged with rape at Nairn and Pitlochry hotels
The man will be put on trial on allegations of raping women in two hotels.
George Webster, 28, was made familiar with the indictment during a hearing at the Glasgow Supreme Court.
He was reportedly due to rape a woman on June 7, 2013 at Scotland's Hotel in Pitlochry, Perthshire.
According to the statement, Webster assaulted her while "she wasn't conscious, asleep and wasn't in a state to give her consent."
Furthermore, Webster is prosecuted for the rape of another woman at the Golf View Hotel in Nairn in the Scottish Highlands of May 4, 2014.
Judge Lady Rae set trial date for November 17 at Edinburgh's Supreme Court
Restoring the very American ideal that labor rights are at the same time human rights
Congressmen Keith Ellison and John Lewis have proposed a law designed to protect organizations in unions as civil law.
"By the way the unions operate, there are jobs for the middle class," says Ellison, a Democratic senator behind Minnesota, who is co-chair of the congressional committee.
That's why I'm so proud that, together with a pattern of occasional rights by John Lewis, I can present a law that empowers employees.
This groundbreaking legislation will give workers the same legal opportunities to defend themselves against discrimination when organizing in trade unions, as with other forms of discrimination - and stop anti-union forces
Adjusting the Labour Relations Act to allow all employees facing discrimination, if they want to get involved in unions, to sue themselves in a civil court - and to assess compensation or damages - is the right and necessary initiative.
But it's certainly not a radical initiative - at least not by American standards.
The best way to understand what Ellison, Lewis, and other petitioners put forward in the law is to re-establish the American ideal.
Despite union struggles that took place in recent years - Wisconsin, Michigan and other states - Americans once supported countries around the world to accept, expand and respect labor rights.
There have been times, and millions of Americans still remember when a country in one breath promoted democracy, freedom of speech, freedom of the press, and the right to assembly.
When the United States occupied Japan after World War II, General Douglas MacArthur and his advisers supported the country to adopt a constitution designed to ensure Hideki Toja's military autocracy was replaced by democracy.
They were convinced that employees and their unions played a role in the formation of the new Japan, included language in them and openly acknowledged that "the right of employees to assemble, collectively negotiate and act is guaranteed."
When the United States occupied Germany after World War II, General Dwight David Eisenhower and his advisers urged the Germans to create a constitution that will ensure that Adolf Hitler's fascism is replaced by strong democracy.
The Germans were aware that employees would need to organize and promote themselves in the new nation, and therefore included a clause that openly says: “The right to create associations that will secure and improve working and economic conditions will be guaranteed to every individual and every profession or profession.
Arrangements that will restrict this right or seek to damage it will be revoked. Measures leading to the fulfillment of such a purpose are unlawful.
When former First Lady Eleanor Roosevelt presided over the International Human Rights Commission, which proposed the Universal Declaration of Human Rights, which was adopted by the United Nations in 1948 as a global treaty, the petitioners and Roosevelt included a guarantee that “everyone has the right, within the protection of their interests, to forge trade unions and enter into them.”
For generations, Americans have embraced the fundamental premise that labor rights are human rights.
When the country advised other countries on how to create a civic and democratic society, Americans explained that the right to create labor unions - and their participation in collective bargaining with firms and government agencies as an equal partner - must be protected.
Now that these rights are being challenged in America, it is definitely necessary to return to the American ideal that working people must have the right to organise themselves in a free and open society.
As Reverend Martin Luther King Jr. said fifty years ago:
History is a great teacher.
Today, everyone knows that unions have not diminished the strength of the nation, but expanded it.
It has raised the living standards of millions of people, created a market for industry, and lifted production to an unsuspected level.
Those who attack the unions forget these simple truths, but history remembers them.
History doesn't forget and we shouldn't, either.
The formal recognition of labour rights as human - and the extension of the protection of civil rights in order to prevent discrimination in the organisation of unions - is very late.
Keith Ellison and John Lewis renew the ideals that have lifted America and made it a true promise of democracy.
A judge has temporarily blocked a law that could close all abortion clinics in Louisiana
A federal judge on Sunday temporarily blocked a Louisianian law that, according to attorneys, would likely close all five clinics conducting miscarriages in the state.
An order enacted by Louisian Governor Bobby Jindal's signature in June, which is due to come into force on September 1, would require doctors performing abortions to have the right to accept a patient in a hospital within 30 miles of their practice.
The judge's order means that doctors can temporarily carry out legal abortions and, in the meantime, have to obtain this permit.
“The proposing party will be able to legally carry out its activities and, in the meantime, obtain permission,” federal judge John deGravelles said.
The hearing will be scheduled within one month so the judge can issue a more permanent resolution regarding the law.
Activists for abortion rights welcomed the resolution, which is the latest in a series of similar measures, and said it would give doctors more time to get permission.
“Today’s decision assures women in Louisiana that they will be safe from a sneaky law to deprive them of their health and their rights,” said Nancy Northup, president and executive director of the Center for Reproductive Rights, who has sought to block the law on behalf of three of three of five clinics.
It was not immediately clear whether the decision also applies to doctors from two clinics that were not among the plaintiffs, and are also requesting this authorisation.
Louisiana is among the eleven states that have passed similar laws, with courts in Alabama and Mississippi declaring such measures unconstitutional.
Key parts of the Texan law that would close most of the remaining clinics in the state were blocked by a federal judge on Friday.
Fighters for women's abortion rights, together with the Association of American Obstetricians and Gynecologists and the U.S. Medical Association, argues that the need to have this permit puts requirements on doctors that are not medically necessary.
Opponents of abortion countered that the measures were intended to protect women's health, and some also welcomed the fact that, because of the measures, clinics would be closed.
The Reproductive Rights Center said only one doctor performing abortions has a permit in Louisiana.
If all doctors in the state were forced to stop performing abortions, this doctor would also stop carrying out the procedure for fear of his safety, the group said.
In response, Louisianian authorities said they would not punish doctors who are carrying out abortion while awaiting the handling of an application for rights.
Due to late diagnosis and poor availability of treatments, ovarian cancer kills in the countryside more often
Angelina Jolie and her brother James posted a video of a tribute to their late mother who died of ovarial cancer in 2007.
There's a higher risk for women in the Australian countryside to die of ovarian cancer than for their counterparts in the city.
The researchers analyzed medical records of more than 1,100 Australians diagnosed with ovarian cancer in 2005 and found that only 35% lived five years after diagnosis.
Head of research Susan Jordan of the QIMR Berghofer Medical Research Institute said that women in rural and remote areas of the state were about twenty percent more likely to die during the study than for women in urban areas.
SMALL STUDY: New drugs can slow lung and ovarian cancer
The women's researchers tracked over seven years of treatment.
Dr. Jordan said the chances of survival affected the age of women at the time of diagnosis, the type of ovarian cancer, other existing diseases and socio-economic status.
The lowest survival rates occurred in older women who were diagnosed with cancer at a more advanced stage.
Women in rural and remote areas of the state were about twenty percent more likely to die during the study than for women in urban areas.
Although the study wasn't designed to determine why out-of-city women were more likely to die from ovarian cancer, Dr. Jordan argues that the factors may be late diagnosis and poor treatment availability.
"The disease is best treated by gynekoonologists, who usually reside in larger cities," she said.
Despite the improvement of long-distance medical services designed to overcome the barrier in the form of distance, the doctor suggested that it would help strengthen the transportation service, which will allow specialists to treat women closer to their homes, and programs to support people who are healing far from their family and friends.
Dr. Jordan said the study revealed that the long-term survival of women with ovarian cancer is low, regardless of the geographical location, which emphasizes the need for better treatment and prevention programs.
The research-funded Rio Tinto Ride to Conquer cancer will be published today by the Medical Journal of Australia.
In March 2012 at the age of 33, a young mum Elisha Neave of Gold Coast was diagnosed with aggressive ovarian cancer.
Garden centers regret a reduction in the number of homeowners
According to a HTA study published by the Financial Times, the decline associated with the extraordinary decline of homeowners under the age of 35 could lead to when today's young generation of consumers reaches the "major age group of gardeners," garden centers will lose up to ten million pounds a year.
According to the report, people who rent housing spend an average of 55% of the amount spent by people living in their own.
As other factors threatening a sector that earns an estimated five billion pounds a year, it cites an increase in the number of people living in very urbanized areas where there are no gardens, the popularity of paving instead of pre-gardens used as a parking area and the shrinking size of gardens.
In the Greater London area, where the proportion of house-owned houses has fallen from 61% to 43% in six years, one household accounts for the lowest expenditure on horticultural products in Britain.
HTA and the Royal Horticultural Society have said that subleasing housing or a lack of space does not mean that people can't grow plants.
Guy Barter, RHS's chief horticultural adviser, said: "For tenants, it's very popular to grow plants in pots - when they move, they can take plants with them."
The HTA report revealed that the "golden age" of the horticultural retail sector was between 1997 and 2005 and was the result of a higher proportion of homeowners and economic prosperity lasting from the 1980s to mid-1990s.
He also predicted higher sales this year thanks to the better weather, which came after unfavourable conditions in March and April.
When an Eastern dictator preaches about democracy.
After Putin, at the reunion of his youth organization, which reminds me most of me about the Hitler Youth Gender, rattled with atomic weapons and basically made it clear to everyone that their views don’t matter to him, he gave us a lecture on democracy today.
But I have a feeling that the current regime in Russia shows many features of a fascist state.
Great nations have one thing in common, and this is that they can be dominated by bothly nationalism, as well as by the fact that inferiority complex can transform them into imperialist appetites.
Time passes differently in the east than in the west.
While the Western world thought that the era of wars for domination over Europe was long gone, in the east, on the contrary, it thinks that they have been overlooked for a long time and it is necessary to regain its international position.
Such a mood and frustration reigned in Germany in the 1930s, just before seizing power by the Nazis, and then followed an outburst of nationalism unlike today's situation in Russia.
And then the completely identical cult of a strong leader, the beating of journalists, the liquidation of inconvenient political opponents, discrimination against minorities and homosexuals, it is quite similar.
Vladimir Putin, and certainly a large part of the Russians, probably misses a time when Russia ruled half of the world and the other part was trembling in front of them.
Western politicians probably caused it on their own, perhaps little Vladimir was patted on his shoulders, maybe they didn't invite him where he longed, and maybe they began to underestimate him, and he simply acted like a typical Russian man.
He made an order at home, pretending he didn't even want to go anywhere, and sharpening knives at home.
Now he's decided he'll regain lost positions.
It starts slow, it has time.
He's probably the leader for life, so where he'd be in a hurry.
Trampled the Caucasians, now he's trampling on the Ukrainians, then it's his turn for the Dwarves from the Baltics.
They've been pinching a Russian bear for a long time.
This will certainly be noticed by China, which is popping up too much and is certainly taking a crush on the far east.
And those Germans also annoy Vladimir how good they are, and we gave them that at 45 on their asses.
How about to make a new Novorosiya in Karlovy Vary.
It would be a base and there are a lot of ours, and as they are being oppressed there, they already wanted to demolish any of them.
After all, our veterans until now will remember how they were in Czechoslovakia.
Nu vot after all, their Prime Minister Gospodin Sobotka, he's so soft, he's going to peel like an onion and I'll eat their whole EU.
That's how it'd play for the big Russia to pop up.
They need to be reminded of their place.
And as detonators, we're going to use our comrades who live on their territory.
There are enough Russians everywhere, and when they say they're oppressed there, they're just oppressing them, and we won't leave that, we'll protect them.
And the Russian will rule again, after all, what else is he born.
After all, Europe is only good as a preposition, on which the proud Ivan can dance a Cossack.
Turkey summons U.S
Turkey's foreign minister called the country's highest-ranking U.S. diplomat to explain a report on U.S. and British espionage activity in Turkey.
Deputy Prime Minister Bulent said U.S. charge d'affaires and Turkish civil servants discussed the report on Monday.
The German magazine Der Spiegel and the online magazine The Intercept reported that according to documents provided by a former analyst of the U.S. National Security Agency, Turkey was a priority target for U.S. and British intelligence services.
According to Turkish media, Turkish President Recep Tayyip Erdogan downplayed the importance of the report and declared that all important states were watching each other.
The earlier report, according to which the German intelligence agency also targeted Ankara, provoked a more indignant reaction from the Turkish government.
Game of Thrones has unveiled other tricks of season four
Game of Thrones creators are currently filming episodes of the 5th orders of the cult series.
At the same time, they boasted about how the tricks of the fourth series were created.
The most visible are computer modifications of the serial environment and the mass scene, when a cluster of soldiers becomes a giant army of the famous Daenerys.
Magaluf police director charged with corruption
Complainants on Friday presented evidence to the Mallor State Prosecutor's Office about extortion by police officers and town hall officials in Calvia's self-government area.
Calvia's director of police on the holiday island of Mallorca has been arrested after charges filed by businessmans and bar owners in the innocatively-famous party district of Magalufu.
Chief Inspector Jose Antonio Navarro was taken into pre-trial detention after allegations of corruption by several businessmen from Punta Ballena, the street in which most bars and nightclubs in Magalufu are located.
According to the online newspaper Mallorca Diario, on Friday the complainants presented evidence to the prosecutor of the Mallorian corruption bureau of extortion on the part of police officers and civil servants of the town hall in the Calvia self-governance area.
Two other local officers have been arrested by Spanish state police because of a link to corruption charges and, along with Navarro, will be interviewed by a judge.
Spain's national ABC newspaper cited dissatisfied owners of nightclaws, according to which favouritism by public officials has a serious impact on their business.
“It’s not about making money anymore, it’s about survival,” one of the entrepreneurs said in court.
Don't mess with our livelihood.
There's nothing to lose.
Magaluf made its way to the main pages of magazines around the world after a video of an 18-year-old BRitish tourist appearing on YouTube who gave oral sex to 24 men during a bar tour.
Since then, island authorities have struggled to oversee drunks and rioters in the ranks of participants in holiday parties in Magaful by reducing the number of infamous alcohol tours in bars.
In addition, the Playhouse club, where there was an event with the provision of oral sex, was forced to close for a year and, together with the organizers of bar tours by Carnage, paid a fine of 55 000 euros (73 000 dollars).
Tourist resort Magaluf, which is especially popular with the British, has also witnessed many accidents under the influence of alcohol, including a fashion wave known as "balconing", in which people jump from one balcony to another or from the balcony to the hotel pool.
The first day of spring marked by rains and thunderstorms that had an impact on a flight at Adelaide airport
SPRING brought an icy surprise to the south of South Australia, coming with heavy rain and fierce winds that seriously hit the Adelaide airport.
Another 5 mm of rainfall in the city should fall until nine o'clock in the evening and another 6.6 mm will fall during the night to Sunday.
The last rain was presented together with a few short showers associated with thunderstorms and gusts of wind, which began before eight o'clock in the evening and in about ten minutes brought almost 4 mm of precipitation.
After several early doses of spring came after the winter of last week, temperatures in the city fell again on Monday to just 15.8°C.
It is believed that the stormy weather has contributed to the delayed arrival of Virgin Airlines from Melbourne to Adelaide.
The plane was supposed to land shortly after 19:30, but at short distances it was hit by a change in speed or wind direction and was forced to postpone the landing.
Gusts of wind at the Adelaide airport at that time reached speeds of about 50 km/h on the ground.
The flight data showed that the plane had to waive the second landing and eventually landed at about 20:40.
The plane's delay subsequently caused delays to additional flights, including an Emirates flight from Dubai and a Qantas flight from Sydney.
According to the forecast, temperatures in Adelaide will reach a maximum of 16°C on Tuesday and there may be one or two showers.
Israeli children return to school after the Gaza war
Thousands of Israeli children from areas close to the Gaza Strip returned to school on Monday after spending the summer in anti-bomb shelters, while rockets and mortars attacked their homes during a fifty-day war between Israel and Hamas. However, schools in Gaza remain closed while the territory rebounds from the fighting.
The beginning of the school brought a sense of joy and excitement to the communities in the south of Israel, but signs of fighting are still noticeable.
In Ashdod, a city in the south of the country, nursery employees hit by a rocket have removed the remains from the wall after sharves and are slowly awaiting the return of children.
"We're a little scared, but we're looking forward to it," said Ronit Bart, a kibbutz resident of Saad and an English teacher at the local school.
A lot of children from this area really need to go back to their daily routine.
Her eleven-year-old daughter Shani Bart revealed that "it's a bit strange" to go to school at once.
"We've been through some difficult moments and we haven't left the house at all," she said.
Kibbutz, located near the border with Gaza, was visited by President Reuven Rivlin to offer residents his support.
As long as the cease-fire last week did not end the war, thousands of residents of border settlements such as Saad remained inside their homes or left their homes and made their way to safer areas further away from Gaza to escape rockets and mortars.
Many Nachal Oz residents, a village near the border with Gaza, in which a missile from a Palestinian mortar killed a four-year-old boy, are hesitating to return.
The education minister said about a dozen families had still not returned.
Their children were temporarily placed in foster schools.
Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu visited a school in Sderot, a city on the border with Gaza, which was severely hit by Palestinian fire.
He urged children to study diligently, adding: "We will provide you with education and safety."
Israel and Hamas agreed last Tuesday on indefinitely.
Stopping the fire led to an immediate end to the fighting, but did not solve the key problems as Hamas's demand to end the Israeli-Gyptian blockade of Gaza and the reopening of Gaza's airports and ports.
Israel is demanding the disarmament of Hamas and the return of the bodies of the two soldiers killed in the war.
A new round of indirect negotiations is expected to begin later this month in Egypt.
According to estimates of Palestinians and the United Nations in the war, more than 2,100 Palestinians died in the war, of which three-quarters consisted of civilians and at least 494 children.
Israel disputes these figures and estimates that at least half of those killed were militants, even though it does not have clear evidence for these claims.
66 soldiers and six civilians, including a Thai worker, have died on the Israeli side.
Hamas and other militants from Gaza fired 4,591 rockets and mortage missiles during fighting on Israeli cities primarily in the south of the country.
In turn, the Israeli army has carried out more than 5,000 air raids and other attacks.
Israeli attacks have damaged or destroyed thousands of homes in Gaza and an estimated 250 000 people have taken shelter in hundreds of U.N. schools that have turned into makeshift shelter.
Due to tens of thousands of people who still remain in hideouts and due to ongoing fighting, school officials postponed the start of the school year last week.
"I hope that the school will be opened soon so that we can complete our education as well as Jewish children and other children in the world," said Mohammad Amara, a thirteen-year-old boy from school in Gaza's largest city.
Vrba at the reunion: I'm standing behind Vácha.
And Lafata's worried about the temperature
The Czech national football team met today before noon in Prague before Wednesday's preparatory match with the USA and the subsequent opening match of the European Championship qualifier against the Netherlands.
From the twenty-three-member cadre, Spartan striker David Lafata, who is troubled by the temperature, has problems.
David isn't here yet, but Sparta had some problems last week, it was more players and David probably still has a bit of problems.
But we want him to be here and okay as soon as possible," coach Pavel Vrba told reporters.
"Whether it's going to be two or three days, or he's going to be fit tomorrow, we'll see according to his medical condition, which will be decided by our doctor," he added.
Other players are fine.
"Obviously, someone has some pain, but it's not like they couldn't train or participate in a match on Wednesday with the U.S."
On Sunday night Vrba was the last player of the Spartan midfielder Lukáš Vácha.
He admitted that it was not an easy decision-making, other midfielders were also considered.
I decided on the basis that Lukas is a prospective player who is 25 years old.
It was very difficult to decide, but I stand behind it," Vrba said to Vácha.
What about Shural?
Let's see what happens to Latata
It is not excluded that in the case of health problems, the nomination could still change.
At the weekend with a great performance flashed, for example, Liberec Josef Šural, who gave a hat-trick.
Of course, there are players who have been showing form recently.
We'll see how David Lafata goes.
In the event that he will not be able to be available on Wednesday, I am not saying that I cannot dominate any of the players who were interested in the Czech league," Vrba said.
This afternoon there will be a training in Strahov, on Tuesday there will be a classic pre-match training session before a duel with the USA at Letná, which will be open for the fans and will be followed by an autograph session.
The match with the Americans is played on Wednesday from 20:15, and the national team in it will strive for the premiere victory under Vrba.
In the previous three preparatory matches, he scored two draws and lost once.
Qualifying clash with the Netherlands is scheduled for 9 September from 20:45.
Pešice is going to the Netherlands in Italy
Vrba is happy to have more time for training, unlike previous reunions.
There's an advantage when you've got players for eight days and you can rehearse certain things with them that maybe we didn't have so much time before.
Now we will be able to focus only on preparing for the Netherlands in the best possible way," Vrba said.
The Dutch will present themselves before the game in Prague on Thursday in Italy and the duel will be personally watched by former interim representative coach Josef Pešice.
He'll give us the information that's from the game.
But it won't just be about him, we'll have a game and we'll be filmed and we'll discuss it.
There's a new coach coming to the Netherlands, we'll see what the changes are going to be there," Vrba said to the bronze medalists from the recent world championship.
According to high-profile statistics, the prices of flats in the winter have risen the most in the last seven years.
According to an index of housing values in the Australian capital RP Data CoreLogic Hedonic, housing prices rose 1.1% in August, the agency said on Monday.
The increase resulted in an overall increase for June, July and August to 4.2%, the highest increase for the winter months since 2007.
However, the annual increase in prices climbed to 10.9%, more than double in one year until August 2013, but the profit was not evenly distributed in the country.
Tim Lawless, director of RP Data's research, said they lead Sydney and Melbourne in the market.
RP numbers Data show Sydney's housing prices rose 16.1% last year, while prices in Melbourne rose 11.7%.
Among the other strongest markets were Adelaide, Brisbane and Darwin, with price rises of an average of between five and six per cent.
On the other side of the scale, Canberra, which was harmed by state-ordered spending reductions and where prices rose only 1.4% during the year.
Lawless said that now that spring has begun, the number of properties for sale will increase in the coming months, which will be a "true test" for the market.
"Due to the persistent high rate of auction sell-offs, generally high sales speeds and low interest rates, the housing value is likely to increase even further over the next three months," he said.
Lenny Henry: My father has never hugged me.
He never told me “I love you”
Henry was born in 1958 as one of the seven children of a Jamaican immigrator in Dudley, Midlands.
They had a cooler relationship with his father, who died when Henry was 19, and who worked at the factory.
Henren rehearses a comedy, Rudy's Rare Records, which is partly based on an imaginary interview with his father and which is part of the Radio 4 series.
The soundtrack is a mixture of reggae and rap, and the melody is optimistic.
Henry, however, had to cope with a few unpleasant childhood memories.
After his mother's death, Henry went through "thoroous" therapies and is now thinking about his relationship with his father.
He knewed very little about him.
You've never seen his face, you've only heard his voice: "Stop the noise.
Leave your sister alone.
Move! Move!
I want to look at cricket.
My older brothers Seymour and Hilton - when I was a kid, were already grown-ups - went to a bar with him and discussed things such as the shape of a beer jar or the beauty of a cricket strike.
I've never spoken to him like that.
Most of my life, he's been a serious guy in a corner reading a newspaper.
Henry had recently visited a foundry in Dudley, and although conditions were better than his father's time, he looked into what life must have been like for him.
There's a little more light there today, but it's still a dark, smoky, gloomy labyrinth where the flames explode and there's a lot of smoke and soot.
Dad always climbed into the tub and was just lying there, then you could hear him slowly starting to sing for himself because he washed off the foundry.
When I walked by, I realized he's been doing this for years to support his family, and I began to value him more.
Even so, Henry spent his childhood without parental love.
Dad didn't hug anyone, didn't say "I love you."
It wasn't until my mom was sick and dying that we started saying to ourselves "I love you, I love you, I love you."
His daughter Billie, whom he has with Dawn French, allowed him to experience the love he lacked as a child.
Can you stop “I love you”?
Don't hug me all the time!
Dad, I'm 22!
With Dawn French.
Why shouldn't we be friends?
She's a great mom
With French, whom he's been married to for 25 years, they're still good friends.
Dawn's nice.
Why shouldn't we be friends with Dawn?
She's a great mother.
Henry's own mother had diabetes.
It was one of the things that killed her.
So when I was very, very overweight and the symptoms of diabetes were starting to show, the doctor told me, “You have to make a radical change.”
So I started going to a large fitness center and I also had to stick to a drastic diet to get rid of symptoms.
It's very hard.
And tiring.
No one wants to eat carrots.
