Kenya registers civil servants to shine a light on non-existent employees
Kenya began biometrically registering all its civil servants to try to remove nonexistent employees from the government's payroll.
According to the government's statement, employees who do not register in the next two weeks will stop receiving a payout.
The government believes that thousands of people receive a salary even after they have left the civil service.
After taking office in 2013, President Uhuru Kenyatta pledged to prevent corruption in government.
An audit carried out earlier this year showed that the state lost at least one million dollars (700 000 pounds) per month on the payouts of non-existent employees and other abuse of official authority.
The government suspects that salaries continue to be deposited into bank accounts even after the employee's death or after an employee leaves the state administration, a BBC reporter from Nairobi's capital, Wanyama Chebusiri, said.
All civil servants must arrive at identification centres within the next two weeks, where their data will be biometrically recorded, according to a government statement.
Furthermore, he says that anyone who does not appear without sufficient apology will be removed from the payroll.
“By designating actual public service employees, this measure will make a significant contribution to its management and will also be used to clear the payroll at both levels of the government to solve the problem of non-existent employees,” said Anne Waiguru, Secretary of the Ministry of Development and Planning.
Dozens of Turkish polices arrested over "conspiracy" against government
Local media reports that a total of 33 policies were detained in Turkey on suspicion of "conspiring against the government."
So far, the police office has not commented on the case.
According to Hurriyet Dail News, fourteen high-ranking officials were detained.
Some of them were embroiled in a December corruption investigation that targeted government officials including four ministers.
In July, several Turkish police officers were arrested for allegedly orchestrating a criminal gang and telephone eavesdropping.
Turkish President Recep Tayyip Erdogan (at the time prime minister) called their actions part of Islamic cleric Fethullah Gülen against him and other people in power.
Not all children have returned to school in Ukraine
Most schools in Ukraine reopened on Monday (September 1) after the summer holidays.
