According to Berkeley, the housing market is returning "to normal"
One of London's most prominent development companies has warned that the housing market has "returned" to a normal level of activity in south-eastern England.
Housing in the capital has been subject to high demand and rising prices, and widespread fears of a credit bubble have prompted the Bank of England to impose limits on mortgages in June.
Tony Pidgley, founder and chairman of the Berkeley-intensive client developer, said on Monday: “Since the beginning of the current financial year, the market has returned from a peak point in 2013 to the normal level of transactions.”
London's housing market fared well during the downturn because buyers from abroad flooded into the capital.
According to data from the Cadastral Office, prices in the city jumped by 18.5% last year, far surpassing the 6.7% average in England and throughout Wales.
Berkeley's average private, affordable and student housing sales prices have risen by about a fifth last year, climbing to 423,000 pounds at the end of April.
However, due to the strengthening pound, they are not so attractive in recent months for foreign buyers of London's properties - some of them have also intimidated the introduction of new property taxes and talk of a possible "settlement tax" that politicians came up with ahead of parliamentary elections next May.
Last week, London-based real estate agency Foxtons warned that April's revision of the mortgage market, which unveiled stricter lending rules, will also slow the market's growth in both property and price sales during the second half of the year.
New data from the Bank of England on Monday showed a decline in the amount of approved mortgages for the month of July, suggesting the housing market is calming.
Hamptons International, another real estate agency, lowered, on the basis that house prices have already begun to fall, its forecast of a rise in the prices of London-based properties for 2015 to 3%.
According to WA Ellis, the volume of transactions has decreased by a quarter each year in London's most expensive neighbourhoods such as Chelsea, Mayfair and Kensington.
Yet demand for housing in the capital was a blessing for Berkeley as it increased cash for forward sales to more than 2.2 billion pounds.
Pidgley added: "The demand for the right, good-looking housing in a good location remained the same and thanks to which the forward sales were sustained."
In June, the company announced that it had sold 3 742 properties from the beginning of the year to the end of April, nearly a third more than before the peak of the 2007 crisis.
Annual pre-tax profit increased by 40% to 380 million pounds, revenue by 18% to 1.6 billion pounds.
On Monday before Pidgley's annual meeting, Pidgley said it anticipated Berkeley's annual returns would be in line with current market expectations.
Analysts agree that annual pre-tax profit will be 450 million pounds.
Berkeley's shares stopped at 23.96 pounds this afternoon on the London Stock Exchange.
