Lesothy kingdom army says no coup is planned ♪ ♪ The prime minister stays in South Africa
Military officers of the Lesoth Kingdom have denied organising a coup to overthrow the government, claiming to have acted against the police suspected of attempting to arm political fanatics.
Prime Minister Thomas Thabane has fled the country, saying the army has surrounded its official mansion and seized government buildings in the capital, Masero.
After declaring they threatened him with death, the prime minister and his family went to neighboring South Africa.
Army spokesman Major Ntlele Ntoi said it wasn't really a coup, but the military's response to a threat from "political fanatics" that police were trying to arm.
"This morning the command of the Defense Forces of the Kingdom of Lesotha acted after receiving several reports of the information service that there are several groups in the ranks of the police that are planning to armament some radical, political fanatics who were about to wreak havoc in the country," he told Voice of America.
A spokesman for the South African government, Clayson Monyela, said military actions looked like an attempt to overthrow the government.
“While no one has declared that he has seized government power through force, the actions of the Lesotho defense forces appear to bear signs of a coup d'état," he said.
Military officials of the Lesotho Kingdom said soldiers returned to their barracks on Sunday and there was calm in the capital.
During Thaban's absence, Deputy Prime Minister Mothetjoa Metsing will be responsible for the government.
Thabane has said that, in his view, he is the target of an attack because of his efforts to fight corruption in the country.
There have been tensions in Lesotho since Thabane suspended the session of parliament due to internal controversies.
He declared that, despite the opposite claims, his actions did not undermine the government.
Production in the eurozone is at a thirteen-month low
Production growth slowed to a 13-month low, according to a closely watched survey in the eurozone in August.
The eurozone's Final Purchasing Managers Index (PMI) fell to 50.7 in August from June's 51.8.
A number greater than 50 indicates expansion.
New orders have declined and factories have been damaged by rising tensions between the EU and Russia over Ukraine.
The figures come from Thursday's meeting of the European Central Bank (ECB).
Markets will want a clear plan from the bank on how to deal with delayed eurozone recovery, as well as the threat of inflation deflation stagnating at 0.3%.
There has been speculation that ECB Director Mario Draghi may have hinted this week that he is considering quantitative easing of the eurozone, similar to what the UK and the U.S. have approached during the financial crisis.
"Although at least some growth is better than no growth, the braking effect that a growing economy and geopolitical insecurity on producers is becoming increasingly visible," said Rob Dobson, chief economist at Markit.
Germany's manufacturing PMI, Russia's largest trading partner in the EU, fell to an 11-month low of 51.4.
In the zone's second-largest economy, France, the PMI fell to 46.9.
There is concern about France, Italy has switched from the expansion of the stagnation.
Signs that the driving force of growth in the most important industrial areas in Germany, Spain and also the Netherlands have disappeared is also not very reassuring," Dobson said.
A slowdown in the industry is likely to pour oil into the fire for analysts awaiting the implementation of further monetary or tax stimulus.
One positive message came from the Republic of Ireland, where PMI has risen to 57.3, the highest level since the end of 1999.
Howard Archer, chief economist at IHS Global Insight, said: "The best that can be said about the purchasing managers' production review for August is that it shows that the sector continues to grow."
