4 tips on how to take better photos and videos underwater
If you are interested in taking pictures or shooting underwater, you have a large selection of equipment.
The cheapest option is a compact such as the Nikon Coolpix AW120 or an outdoor camera type GoPro Hero3+ Silver Edition, which costs about 300 dollars.
I took these photos at a family celebration of several cameras, all of which cost less than 350 dollars.
It doesn't matter what equipment you use, but a few rules apply for the best results.
Re-check the equipment.
Even if you have a waterproof camera, make sure the batteries and other parts are firmly sealed.
Next, adjust the camera according to what type of photos or videos you will take.
Some cameras and video cameras have a scenic or shooting mode, which optimizes the exposure for a dark underwater environment.
And before you get into it, find out how deep you can dive with your equipment.
Some cameras are only up to 5 feet, another up to 50 to 60.
Read our guide to the purchase and evaluation of digital cameras in normal and waterproof versions.
Always take a few pictures - a lot of them won't come out.
Compact cameras have an LCD that will help you with the composition of photos, but the outdoors do not have it.
Even if you have an LCD, it will be difficult to see underwater, and the composition of the shot will be unpredictable.
Therefore, always take more pictures.
If the camera has a bracketing mode that does a series of images with a slightly different dispersion of exposure, take advantage of it.
Stay close to the surface.
With a deeper dive of light, it decreases significantly.
If possible, stay close to the surface when shooting in a swimming pool, lake or sea.
It also allows you to capture more light in the photos - the deeper you sink, the fewer colors you will see.
Get close to the subject being photographed.
That's excellent advice if you're taking pictures on land, but it's even more important underwater because of the muffled light.
It’s especially important if you’re filming with an outdoor camera: Such devices usually have a solid and wide lens angle, which means that if you want to fill a photo frame, you need to get closer to objects.
Andrew Lawson was a man whose power of personality was able to change things even in such a huge organization as the NHS.
As an anesthesiologist, he dedicated his career to relieving patients from the pain caused by illness and treatment.
His wife recalls that one of the people who sought his help was an MI6 officer who had to live with the aftermath of torture that maimed him.
Lawson understood that while doctors focused on diagnoses and illnesses, treated patients overwhelmingly were interested in something completely different: pain.
One day in 2007, however, he was the one who began to suffer.
"I don't feel myself," he wrote in May that year.
I felt with no energy.
He struggled with flu symptoms and found he was impatient and his wife Juliet included regrets.
He said, “I want everything to happen sooner rather than later.”
When Juliet went on a business trip for a week, Lawson was unusually and inexplicably upset.
Something wasn't right.
A colleague gave him an X-ray of his chest.
For one or two weeks before, he skied in the French Alps.
X-ray results have come.
He had a mesothelioma, an incurable tumor that affects the pleura or lining of the lungs.
For most types of cancer, it's difficult to determine what was their cause.
Although some smokers get lung cancer, not all lung cancer patients have smoked.
Mesothelioma is different.
In almost all cases, the cause of exposure to asbestos - a building fibrous material that was formerly referred to as "miraculous", but today it is known that it is deadly dangerous.
It wasn't hard for most of us to ignore mesothelioma.
Asbestos, after all, belongs to the past.
The most dangerous type of asbestos has not been used in Britain since the 1960s, when the ban on the use of asbestos in the industry came into force.
Even at a time when asbestos was being used, only certain groups of people came into close contact with it, for example, insulating layers, builders, carpenters and workers in shipyards.
The toxin used in industry in a different era now finds it hard to seem a cause for concern.
Such complacency, however, is not appropriate.
As it turns out, a mesotheliomal epidemic culminates in Britain today.
The number of deaths from mesothelioma is higher here than in any other country in the world.
2,500 deaths from the disease a year are twice as many as the death toll from car accidents.
The number of mesothelioma deaths a year since 1980 and estimated deaths in the future in the UK
The reason we are only now experiencing its effects, even though the use of asbestos has been illegal for years (all kinds of asbestos were eventually banned by law in 1999), is that it usually takes several decades for mesothelioma to develop.
Disaster in the form of mesothelioma does not only affect veterans of the construction industry.
Asbestos was, and in many cases is still present, in the homes in which we live, in the offices where we work, in the schools where we learn, and in the shops in which we shop.
Mesothelioma therefore disrespects the social class, wealth, vocation or age.
Privileged bastions ranging from London-based department stores to public schools don't represent a hideout.
Parliament, too, is full of asbestos.
Even hospitals in which they should treat us are a repository of this deadly carcinogen.
Andrew Lawson wasn't old.
He didn't even consider isolation.
He was trying to figure out where he could come into contact with asbestos.
Then he figured it out.
"It's possible that a lot of asbestos has been in Guy's Hospital basement corridors, where I've spent six years to practice," he wrote.
All - students, nurses, doctors and doors - used the hallways.
The question is, how many of my contemporaries get the same disease?
Unfortunately, it was a question he was able to partially answer.
"Of the four physicians who were at Guy's Hospital and who had a mesothelioma over the past five years," he remarked in a 2010 letter, "I'm the only one I'm still alive."
How many of us get sick?
Andrew Lawson was diagnosed with mesothelioma when he was 48.
When he died on February 17 this year, he was 55.
Surviving with this diagnosis for so long is not usual.
55 percent of people diagnosed with mesothelioma die within eight months of diagnosis.
Illness is always deadly.
Now, therefore, we can only repeat Lawson’s question: “Which of us gets sick with the same disease?”
According to leading British mesotelioma expert Professor Julian Pete, the best estimate is that between 1970 and 2050, when the asbestos epidemic in Britain takes place, about 90,000 people will die.
Most of them currently have no idea in this way.
Asbestos mine in Quebec, Canada
Taking a quick look at the files from the archives of the courts, to which the disabled often turn and require compensation, it shows how much the mesotheliomic disaster has spread.
For example, Marks & Spencer acknowledged this June for negligently exposing Janice Allen to asbestos.
She worked for the chain for nine years, from 1978 to 1987, when she oversawed the clothing section in two stores - one of the venues was the company's flagship, an Oxford Street store.
Allen was only 18 when she started working for M&S.
Today he has two children with over twenty.
“Before it happened,” she says, “I’ve never heared of mesothelioma before, I barely knew anything about asbestos.
It never occurred to me that he might concern me somehow.
There aren't many people who know anything about asbestos.
In fact, asbestos is not one substance, but a group of six minerals.
He was given the name due to his fibrous structure, which gives him strength and flexibility.
Of the six minerals, three were used in the construction industry.
Chryzotile, commonly known as white asbestos, can be found in buildings today.
It has been used in roof panels, floor tiles, boiler seals and even brake linings.
It's not as deadly as other forms of asbestos, the EU and WHO nonetheless consider it a "great health risk" that can cause death.
However, brown asbestos (amosite) and blue asbestos (crokydolite) are more dangerous.
Britain was once the world's largest importer of brown asbestos, and experts say "there is conclusive but circumstantial evidence that this was the leading cause of the unusually high occurrence of mesothelioma [in the United Kingdom]."
Marsk & Spencer has been exposed to asbestos at a flagship store in Oxford Street, London
Janice Allen may not consider herself a typical victim of mesothelioma, but Julian Pete's work points out that her story isn't unusual at all.
His patient study argues that “a substantial proportion of mesothelioma cases without known exposure to asbestos at work or at home were probably caused by exposure to asbestos in the environment.”
A significant part of this exposition is due to the ‘common use and weathering’ of buildings.
It seems that no one can be sure he's safe.
The Goddard Consulting report, which surveyed the Palace of Westminster, shows that even the people in the middle of the government may not suspect they may be exposed to asbestos.
In 2009, Goddard published a report saying service elevators and pipes behind the parliamentary committee's rooms were contaminated with asbestos whose deadly fibers could be breached by something as harmless as "strong airflow."
Ministers are often accused of taking care only of their own interests, but in this case the opposite may be true.
Although Parliament's Services Directorate insisted on getting a "positive medical certificate," it is now envisaged that one million pounds and several years of work would be needed for Parliament to be thoroughly inspected, to modernise electricity and to remove asbestos, and that, after the 2015 elections, MPs would sit in the premises of the Queen Elizabeth II Conference Centre rather than on the green benches in Westminster.
Goddard's report states that "the presence of asbestos does not comply with several regulations."
It is impossible to say whether this discrepancy will cost lives.
Now everyone can only wait.
One of those who have never been able to determine when they experienced exposure to asbestos is Graham Abbott, a doctor.
Like Andrew Lawson, Abbott (50) suspects he was exposed to asbestos while working in hospitals.
"I worked in a hospital where the presence of asbestos has been shown," he says, "but I can't prove it in my case.
It's difficult to remember all the places where he worked and when he worked there.
However, he clearly remembers the day at the beginning of December 2009, when he was defeated by something like chills.
He was 45, he was in the evening, and he was in the middle of surgery.
All of a sudden I trembled.
It came at once.
I felt terrible.
I didn't think I'd get home by car.
Because he was a doctor, he knew that the pain was coming from the pleura, the lining around his lungs.
But, like Janice Allen, he had no reason to believe that it was a mesothelioma.
He eventually spent a month at home.
Confused doctors gave him a chest X-ray and introduced his thoracic catheter to remove the fluid they had sent from his lungs for analysis.
Still, they didn't make a diagnosis.
His condition slowly improved and he returned to work.
From time to time, however, his symptoms came back, often after he exercised and breathed.
In 2011, one of Abbott's patients with similar symptoms came to the operating room and was subsequently diagnosed with mesothelioma.
Even then, Abbott didn't associate anything with his own case.
After all, his patient was several decades older and came into direct contact with asbestos at work.
In this case, the connection was clear.
In September 2011, Abbott's condition deteriorated again, and his doctor consulted on his CT scan and X-ray with a panel of experts.
In December 2011, exactly two years after Abbott began to feel ill, a probe equipped with a camera was introduced into the space between the lining of the thoracic cavity and the lining of his lungs.
I'm an optimist.
I've got a tendency not to take too much," he says.
To be honest, I didn't worry too much about it.
But Rachel, my wife, was worried.
Biopsy results arrived between Christmas and New Years: "They told me it was a mesothelioma."
Graham Abbott: diagnosed with mesothelioma
Abbott began to meet with nurses from the charity Macmillan, and one advised him to contact a lawyer.
That's when he realized the extent of the epidemic.
"It turns out that asbestos has been widely used, primarily in large public buildings, which often have asbestos thermal insulation in pipelines," he says.
People who have been exposed to asbestos in these buildings are now beginning to show the disease.
Mesothelioma is now starting to concern younger people who do not have high-risk vocations.
The most dangerous asbestos-insulated pipes were found in hospitals in the basement, so it was unlikely to endanger patients.
However, members of the staff, who passed through basement corridors between buildings (like Andrew Lawson) or ate in basement canteens (as often Graham Abbott) certainly came into contact with a toxic substance.
As it now turns out, several decades after the war, hospitals were a place where they could save patients' lives, but also a place that could endanger the lives of the doctors who treated them.
Asbestos is removed to this day.
Pupils put Bunsen's kahan on asbestos pads
And it's not just hospitals.
Asbestos was often used in offices, shops, libraries and town halls because of its excellent insulating and non-flammable properties.
In schools, too.
In fact, many asbestos people have already been exposed at school.
Somewhere in countless chemistry classes, students were building Bunsen's kahan on an asbestos pad.
A website dealing with asbestos in schools was created.
Harmless items such as floor tiles or the roofs of tool shelters in our homes commonly contained asbestos.
“It’s an industrial poison built into a large number of houses,” notes Andrew Morgan, a lawyer who represented Andrew Lawson in his lawsuit against Guy’s Hospital.
In one case, there was a single contact with asbestos, which a woman suffering from mesothelioma was able to remember, in the 70's during the destruction of the garden house.
So be careful how you destroy the garden house.
Knowing that the disease is incurable, the impact of the diagnosis is enormous.
“It takes a while for you to figure it out,” says Graham Abbott.
I went back to work and tried to carry on, but found I couldn't focus on what I was doing.
I've been in the operating room for two weeks.
Then I realised I'd have to leave and deal with myself.
Well, I won't live to see another Christmas.
One of the most difficult things was the transition from a doctor's position to a patient position.
As a myriad of patients before him, he also remembers how confused he felt about the amount of information he had to deal with.
“It was difficult to understand everything,” he says.
“I asked my doctor, “How much time do I have?”
I've been told that twelve months.
I remember thinking, “Well, I won’t live to see another Christmas.
That's the way it is.'
Mesothelioma is particularly destructive, because the very mechanism of what keeps us living - breathing itself - is what causes cancer that kills.
Most mesothelioma cases are caused by exposure to asbestos.
Asbestos consists of small threads.
When asbestos breaks and the fibers inhale, they can settle in pleus, the lining of the lungs.
Asbestos fibers irritate the pleury and can trigger cell mutations.
"The problem is inhaled asbestos fibers in the shape of needles," explains Professor Tom Treasure, a cardiac surgeon who joined Guy's Hospital in 2001.
To the same hospital where Andrew Lawson was apparently exposed to asbestos and which, paradoxically, is a leading center in mesothelioma treatment.
