Two-and-a-half years after his own diagnosis, Graham Abbott is still struggling.
After discovering the mesothelioma, Abbott made contact with Andrew Lawson, who, four years after his diagnosis, became an individual support and adviser to equally disabled people.
When they called, he cheerfully introduced himself: "Hello, you've reached the cancer headquarters."
“He’s been very positive,” Abbott says.
He was diagnosed with the disease four years ago and he was still very active.
Initially, Abbott was offered six cycles of chemotherapy, which would last four months, and would probably extend his life by only a month.
"I was desperate," he says.
I wanted to give up.
Lawson, however, "has been able to show things in a more optimistic light."
After attending several doctors, Abbott decided to resume treatment with Professor Loic Lang-Lazduski from the chest surgery department at Guy's Hospital.
“I had the advantage that I didn’t have to wait for them to send me to him, but I just called there, and they examined me,” Graham admits.
A normal patient would have to get a requisition and they would have to approve his reimbursement.
In order to access the best treatment available, the question of money is crucial for mesothelioma patients.
But when various treatments inevitably fail, many patients face further financial concerns - the future of the family they leave behind.
And that's why they're turning to the courts and they want compensation.
Andrew Lawson has contacted Andrew Morgan of Field Fisher Waterhouse LLP.
“Since 1898, asbestos has been known to be detrimental to health,” Morgan said.
However, in the 60's it was found that even a very small amount of it could pose a health risk.
And here we encounter the negligence of companies.
Andrew Lawson and Guy's Hospital eventually coped, but according to Morgan, it wasn't a "full-fledged settlement" because Lawson couldn't definitively prove that mesothelioma was a result of exposure to asbestos at Guy's Hospital.
After an investigation into the case, however, Guy's Hospital spokesperson confirmed that "the asbestos in the incriminated basement section was removed in the 1990s."
It was too late for Andrew Lawson.
How to remove asbestos
Seducing exposure to deadly asbestos to one business or a place of work - usually decades after this happened - has proved to be a huge problem for mesotheliome-stricking mesothelioma patients.
A lot of former workplaces changed owners or went bankrupt.
Evidence of insurance has been lost.
And those who are now defending against the charges have time on their side, which their prosecutors certainly don't.
In response to this fact, an important new legislation has been passed this year, which makes it easier for mesotheliomal patients to seek compensation, even if it is not possible to trace their former employers.
The law has created a 350-million-dollar financial reserve paid for by insurance companies that is intended for people diagnosed after July 2012, who can prove that they have been exposed to asbestos, but have no one to sue.
In such a case, the victims will receive 80% of the amount that, in a normal case, the court would attribute to them as compensation - around 120 000 pounds.
300 successful applications are expected each year.
Andrew Morgan, like mesothelioma patients, think 350 million pounds is a "good job."
"It's an agreement written by insurance companies for insurance companies," he says, pointing out that the sum is a quarter of what insurance companies would have had to pay if time hadn't intervened and people with mesothelioma would be able to track down former employers and sue them in a normal way.
Even Mike Penning, a former secretary of labor and social affairs, acknowledged the law "had gaps."
But both Penning and Morgan admit that, given seven deaths each year, swift action was necessary.
"People are suffering too much and they need help right away," Penning said in December last year during a second reading of the law on mesothelioma.
