The operation was followed by radiotherapy and chemotherapy - a triple treatment in which Lang-Lazdunski boasts a survival rate of five years in up to 40% of patients.
Abbotta strengthened it.
“Of course, that’s not the most important thing,” Abbott says.
In patients, you see it all the time.
There's something that drives you.
If you give up, your condition can deteriorate very quickly.
Graham Abbott underwent surgery in March 2012.
At the end of August, he completed the last of six cycles of chemotherapy.
The check-ups showed no symptoms of the illness.
I went for another check-up in March [2014].
There were a few stains of [cancers] scattered in my chest.
My 50th birthday was just approaching.
It doesn't endanger life.
It's taking him.
Abbott underwent six cycles of chemotherapy again.
There are currently no traces of a tumor in his body.
But it's a physically and emotionally exhausting process.
You have to think about practical things - financial security when I die, or to show a woman how an alarm clock on a cauldron works.
When you learn bad news, you start to be negative.
You have to look forward.
It's not always easy for Ellie's father (16) and Tamsin (14).
“It’s hard for parents,” he says.
It's hard to decide what to say and how much to say.
After the diagnosis, I told the girls that my condition means I'll never be old.
Their reaction was different.
Tamsin is very social and wild.
She told friends, and all of a sudden a lot of people started calling us.
Ellie was more restrained.
She didn't say much.
Such conversations must be faced by all cancer patients.
In patients with mesothelioma, however, such conversations aren't eased by hope or just a hint of a chance of survival.
The disease brings (and, in the end, it has brought joyless certainty to Stephen Jay Gould).
As Andrew Morgan says, “mesothelioma doesn’t jeopardize life.”
