China refuses to give Hongon the power to elect leadership - protesters promise revenge
China's parliament, despite increasing discussions on democratic reform on Sunday, decided not to allow voters in Hong Kong to nominate candidates for the 2017 elections.
It is likely that the move will provoke long-promised protests in Hongon's business district, as activists began to organize and prepare just hours after the announcement.
China's People's Congress essentially permits communist leaders to eliminate any candidate who is not loyal to Beijing.
"We weren't surprised, but we're infuriated," lawmaker Emily Lau, chairwoman of the Democratic Party, said.
That's not what Beijing promised.
They lied to people in Hong Kong.
And it's clear we're dealing with an authoritarian regime.
In defense of China's decision, the deputy secretary-general of the Permanent Committee of the National People's Congress, Li Fei, said that allowing public nominations in Hong Kong's leadership elections would cause "chaos."
Since 1997, when Britain returned China's administration to Hong Kong, Beijing has vowed that residents will be able to vote for executive power as of 2017.
Chinese leaders presented Sunday's decision as a democratic breakthrough because it allows Hong Kong a direct vote, but the decision also makes it clear that Chinese leaders are holding firmly in their hands the committee's nominating process, which is tightly controlled by Beijing.
And according to the new clause, only candidates who "love the country and Hong Kong" will be permissible.
The decision came after the summer months, which saw some of Hong Kong's biggest and most-watched protests in several years.
Most pro-democratic protests in Hong Kong are behind the Occupation of the Headquarters with Love and Peace, whose organizers threatened to close a financial neighborhood if Beijing did not grant them universal suffrage.
On Sunday evening, just hours after the announcement, hundreds of supporters of the Occupation movement in the rain gathered in front of Hong Kong government headquarters.
At the demonstration, the organizers said that their movement was entering a new phase of civil disobedience and that in the coming weeks it would start waves of protests.
However, they did not provide any further details, apparently wanting to avoid problems with the authorities.
In an online statement, the organizers said that the movement had "considered the occupation of the Headquarters only as a final step if all possibilities of dialogue were exhausted and there would be no other choice."
We regret to announce that all possibilities of dialogue have been exhausted today, and the occupation of the Headquarters is inevitable.
Authorities in Hong Kong have been preparing for Beijing's announcement for days, boosting the government's seat protection on Sunday, deploying police officers and barricades.
According to many Hong Kong residents, inciting riots is a sign that it is slowly losing control of the city.
The influx of people from the mainland increases competition in the field of products and services.
There are also growing fears that values such as democracy and freedom of speech are bent by increasing pressure from Beijing.
Some people have criticized the Occupation of the Headquarters movement and say that their demonstrations threaten Hong Kong's traffold.
“The protest he’s talking about could cause much more economic damage depending on how many people participate and for how long,” said Regina Ip, a politician who has long criticized the movements.
We don't want fears that Hong Kong is getting out of control.
Such a perception could harm investment.
China's state media has also come forward in recent days with reports portraying Democratic activists in Hong Kong as subversive agents controlled by the West.
In the summer, activists organized an unofficial referendum on voting rights, with 780 000 participants - more than one-fifth of Hong Kong voters.
And in July, tens of thousands of people took part in one of the largest pro-democratic demonstrations in the area's history.
Pre-Election agita?
