The American tradition penetrates into university life - freshmen can fly a jet
We are delighted to be able to respond to the demand by launching the first luxury travel service for students in Britain.
In order to leave the best possible impression on arrival at the university, the company also offers transportation options including a private jet or Rolls-Royce Phantom, Aston Martin or McLaren P1.
Stewart also said that the service also has a security side.
Service is an ideal option for students who would otherwise have to drag their belongings across the state in a dangerously loaded car.
We look forward with our VIF (very important freshman) options this year to ensure students arrive at the university with minimal nervousness and maximum luxury.
The company's spokesperson said that since the services have only just been launched, they don't have any reservations yet, but added: "Students will start making bookings within the next few weeks."
The company also said that although students pay an annual tuition fee of 9,000 pounds, it expects the service to find its place in the market.
Compared to students ten, twenty, thirty or forty years ago, today’s students are different in terms of expectations and wishes – it’s more important than ever to make the right first impression, and the VIF program is exactly the right way.
However, the National Union of Students criticised the service as unattainable.
Megan Dunn, Vice President of the Union for Higher Education, noted: "For most students, such a project is unattainable.
Many of the students who arrive at university this month have to cope with the financial crisis before throwing thousands of pounds for something as simple as arriving at a dormitory, because affordable financial support in the form of loans and grants is unable to keep pace with the rising cost of basic needs.
Feminists oppose racism and police behaviour after Ferguson events
At the beginning of the month, an officer in Ferguson, Missouri killed an unarmed youth.
Those waiting for a funeral with their hands up chanting: "Hands up, don't shoot!"
After two weeks of protest in Ferguson, Missouri over the shooting death of unarmed younger Michael Brown, blogger Miriam Zoila Peréz has seen a shift in online discussions of white feminists.
She found that when it comes to promoting reproductive rights and income equality, white feminists prioritize gender over race.
With rising tensions in the Midwest that drew the attention of the national press, Peréz noticed that white women's reactions centered 100% on racism.
Compared to the reactions of black women soon after the shooting on August 9, personal articles with headlines such as "thinking of a white woman over Ferguson" or "Feminism is not just about oppressing women" appeared relatively late.
According to Peréz, however, this represents an important change.
“I don’t think there are many people in the feminist movement who talk about race and privileges at that level,” she said.
People felt they had to comment on what had happened.
It's characteristic of the crisis we're facing, because [the Ferguson situation] is so racially defined that they had to draw attention to it.
According to Koritha Mitchell, an English professor at Ohio State University, Ferguson has brought to light the problems African-American women have to face every day and are not seen as “female issues” in the cultural realm.
"I can post funny stories about my life on Facebook with my partner and it'll get 150 likes from people from all over the world," Mitchell said.
When I write something about the fact that colors are trapped in their own land, it's silence deafening.
"How is it possible for a woman to talk publicly about relationships, but not about security in a public space?" she asked.
For black women such as Mitchell, who dealt with the issue of race, gender and sexuality in American history, there is no dichotomy among the issues of race and gender.
According to her, black women couldn’t indulge in the luxury of clearly dividing these problems: they live in this mixed reality every day.
Although whites now merge the issue of race and gender in mainstream feminist circles, Angela Hattery, a female lecturer and gender studies professor at George Mason University, says their predecessors did the opposite.
Between 1865 and 1890, at least 10 000 blacks were lynched, and the reason was, in almost all cases, the rape of a white woman," Ms Hattery said.
To justify the lynching, you needed the cooperation of a white woman.
According to Hattery, the split between white women and black girls came when a campaigner for the right to vote like Susan B. Anthony examined the terrain at the end of the 19th century and realized that only one group of people - women or black people - could have the right to vote.
"They have decided to bet cards on women's voting rights and focus on the right for black people later," Ms Hattery explained.
When we realize this, we get an important insight into why colored women don't trust white women.
We didn't do a good job.
We've never helped black women protect their husbands and children.
Even after women gained the right to vote in 1920, it took ten years for them to start organizing against lynching at the Association of Southern Women to Prevent Lynching.
The group was formed 40 years after black women first asked for help.
According to Mitchell's surge of responses from white feminists to the issues raised in Ferguson - the question of police behavior, racial discrimination - reflects a time when whiteen feminists had to "catch up" on the issues that black women have been dealing with for generations.
“Wouldn’t it be great if activists in these spaces [traditional feminism] took the right to nurture a child as seriously as they took the right to contraception?” Mitchell said.
The second wave of feminists who have made a career fighting for reproductive law and access to contraception are now finding out what it means.
Prominent feminist activists like Gloria Steinem took two weeks after Michael Brown's shoute by attacking Facebook, where they posted a column by Rebecca Carroll of the Guardian, which demanded that white Americans protest more vigorously against racism.
"I hope that women who have different yet similar reasons for understanding the dangers in society - and opinions about race that varies significantly in opinion polls - will strive for change," Steinem wrote.
Although Steinem advocates intersectional feminism, which includes both race and gender issues, her comments on the issue of race in America confuse some people.
Christina Sommers, author of “Who Stole Feminism?”, told She The People that young men in the United States, especially young colored men, are “much more vulnerable than their sisters,” but Remarges by Ferguson’s Steinem parties has come under criticism earlier.
We now have hundreds of special programs for girls and young women, but almost none for boys.
But when the White House launched a small My Brother's Keeper program designed to help vulnerable young Negroes and Hispanics, it sparked an angry reaction from many feminists, including Gloria Steinem.
Police's relationship with the black community is not the only issue that comes at the forefront of mainstream feminism.
After U.S. border police detained nearly 63,000 unaccompanied juveniles in the country's southwest this year, the issue of reforming immigration laws - this time as a women's question, has arisen again.
Andrea Mercad, co-chairing We Belong Together, an organisation mobilizing women to reform immigration laws, said that in order to present the question as necessary for women, all she had to do was share immigrant stories.
"They speak for themselves," Mercad says.
Since most work permits are granted to men, women who come up with them find themselves in a position where they are vulnerable in terms of domestic violence and abuse.
“These stories have a response in women’s organizations,” she said.
