An oral agreement with an employer can be a problem
Unfortunately, you didn't have a certificate of incapacity from the doctor, which serves as evidence for the organization, that you can't perform work duties for medical reasons.
In addition, you did not immediately report the absence to your employer.
Subsequently, there was an agreement that the given days would be registered as a holiday, but the agreement took place only verbally.
Although the Labor Code does not impose an obligation to have the employer’s consent to take leave confirmed in writing, in practice this procedure has proven successful.
If there is only an oral agreement, you do not have confirmation of the agreement.
Later, the alleged consent to the drawing of leave, as well as in your case, can be denied and the absence at work assessed as a long-term unexcused absence, hence the reason for the immediate termination of the employment relationship pursuant to § 53 of the Labour Code.
In the event of litigation about an agreement with your employer, you lack demonstrable evidence.
However, the employment relationship with you has not been cancelled according to § 53, only you have the given days listed in the credit card as an unexcused absence.
Unexcused absence in the credit certificate does not have the same consequences as § 53 (e.g. impossibility to receive material unemployment security), yet it makes it more difficult to find a new job.
We recommend that you visit the doctor again and have it confirmed in writing that there was actually an injury, due to which you were incapacitated to work (this is not a so-called "incompetence", it is only a doctor's proof).
When looking for a new job, you can submit this document and explain the situation.
In practice, it is advisable not to underestimate both the issuance of a written medical certificate of incapacity for work and an early notification to the employer or the request for written consent to take leave.
You will avoid possible complications.
