The fired employee took revenge, bought laptops at the company and sold them
The disgruntled employee, after his release, bought computers at the company and then sold them.
He managed to spend the money before his arrest by the police.
In a short period of time, the man bought a total of sixteen computers for more than two hundred thousand crowns.
"He took the laptops to Olomouc, where he sold them in bazaars," said Rostislav Brückner, chief commissioner of the Šumperk police.
The motive for the scams was vengeance to the employer for dismissal and also the profit of money.
"During the interrogation, he told us that he used the money to repay the loans," Brückner said.
Police also discovered that the fraudster was a pathological player who had been treated repeatedly for psychiatry.
Thus, the money could also end up in slot machines or in video lottery terminals.
"The fact is that we didn't find any money with him," the commissioner said.
A scammer who has already been punished for theft is now facing between one and five years in prison.
Police have not yet disclosed whether there is also a threat of punishment for bazaars, which bought the laptops below the price and continued to sell them.
"It's true that they've taken over completely new goods, which is at least suspicious," the commissioner said.
However, none of the bazaar's owners have yet been charged.
Come on, just a bit more, you can do it or how I enjoyed We Run Prague
This year, the organizers have prepared a few changes for us.
The biggest one was the change of route, which brought the destination to the waterfront, rather than leading back to the Yellow Spa.
I was looking forward to the race together with the rest of the Rungo.cz team, but I was going to experience it a bit from the other side.
With the whole team, we decided to personally greet our readers at the stand, where we took pictures and shooting videos together.
In order to bring my view from the other side to perfection, I accepted the role of a personal conductor.
Together with my friend Kát'a, we obediently ranked at the end of the corridor for 60 to 70 minutes just before the start.
We greeted with our friends, who walked past us to the sector for faster runners.
There wasn't much time left.
A few minions to warm up, turn on sports testers, countdown and start!
Creaks of cheers, but instead of a quick start-up, it was followed by squeezing towards the starting line.
After five minutes we reach the gate, turn on the measurements and run at a pace for an hour of ten.
We weave through the yellow snake of runners, who, like Kát'a, run their first race.
So far, they all look fresh, the slowly declining sun in the sky adds an almost cheesy tinge to the view of Vyšehrad.
Pulsing meat stretching along the embankment looks as if the whole of Prague is running here.
Traditionally, on the embankment between Vyšehrad and the National Theatre, it is beginning to show who has how much strength.
A light scoop on Palacký Square forces many runners to slow down or walk.
Just before running over to Divadelní I see a police accompaniment and right behind him Vítek Pavlišta flies at an incredible pace to the finish.
I hear the admiring sighings of other runners.
I'm moving to the edge of the track, I'm cheering on it, it's going to slap with me, but just before that my leg sprains and our hands narrowly miss.
Revolting Divadelní Street gives a chance for a short rest and breathing.
It is necessary, there are places in front of us, where the views will take my breath away.
The turn on the Mánes Bridge shows Prague in all the beauty of the setting sun.
I don't breathe, and my feet carry me almost by themselves.
Behind the first refreshment comes a moment of truth.
During the crossing of the road, Kát'a stumbles against the curb and performs a swim jump.
I'm looking to see if she needs help, and I'm launching a series of encouraging words so she doesn't have time to think about pain.
It rises and we continue through Kozí aisle, it is one of the narrowest points of the track, at this stage of the race there is not such a rush, but it is still necessary to monitor the surroundings well.
I don't know yet that the concept of the narrowest place will gain its true meaning later.
Behind the Dusty Gate, we are frenetically encouraged by tourists and friends of other runners.
I'm trying to applaud with everyone who offers their hand, and I'm advisinging Kata, too, because ten clapping with fans is like half an energy bar.
It really works, we're accelerating again, and even though we've fallen quite significantly, now the pursuit has begun.
We're overtaking dozens of runners when I'm passing someone particularly tired, I'm trying to encourage him with a few words: "Come on, just a little more, you can do it!"
There's no one of us for the last refreshment, we're accelerating.
So far we've been running for an hour and a quarter, we're adding, there's nothing to wait.
Three kilometers to the finish will hurt, but you really need to work.
The sun has slowly set down and the end of the day is symbolically slowly ending the race.
Last run to the bridge, a turn and just a straight to the finish.
The pace we're running amazes me, where's that girl coming from?
"Come on, we're going under an hour thirteen, we're working!" I call.
We're trying it, we're sprinting and there's a goal.
Thirteen and six seconds.
Well, what can be done.
The coach entered a limit of hour twenty.
So seven minutes below schedule.
That's a great result.
Just behind the goal, Kata's legs are undermined, I'm helping her to the railing, where she's been resting for a while.
It shows exhaustion, but also happiness from a well-done performance.
After a while, I'm slowly taking her out of the finish area.
Euphoria doesn't give us any thought of pushing in the passageways to the locker rooms.
You can see here that Goat Lane wasn't the narrowest place.
That's all the way here behind the goal of the riverbank, which is under the onslaught of almost ten thousand runners stuffed to bursting.
If someone doesn't like crowds, it's not a place for him.
After a while, I also lose my head and wonder if the wonderful views and compared to previous years the more interesting route was worth what was going on at the destination.
From my own experience, I know it's no fun.
If ten thousand people are added to it, a rich accompanying program and the closure of the whole center of Prague for all traffic, this is a huge challenge.
This year's change of the route showed the runners many interesting places, but it also brought some really unpleasant surprises.
An excellent atmosphere pumped in the Yellow Spa was transferred between the runners even in the race itself.
Organizers around the track and random spectators escalated it on every meter of the track.
Unfortunately, during the city run through the historical centre, obstacles that cannot be eliminated must be counted, but I do not consider the target of the mass race in the place, which on the one hand is bounded by a wall and on the other by a river.
If I tell myself that the race ended with the finish, I rate it as very successful.
I was a little worried about the health of those involved, but in the end everything went well.
Write us in the discussion, if you liked the route and what do you think about the situation at the destination.
Prison guards in Bulgaria organise protests
Hundreds of prison service employees across Bulgaria participated in a nationwide protest rally in front of the Ministry of Justice in the capital Sofia.
At a peaceful demonstration, they reiterated their demand for the reintroduction of the original working conditions, which have recently changed.
Higher salaries are also on the list of demands to be discussed withing interim Justice Minister Hristo Ivanov.
For a month, wardens symbolically protested against new working hours and demanded a return to their original twenty-four-hour shifts.
Although unions of prison guards and representatives of the Ministry of Justice met, they did not agree, the Bulgarian State Radio (BNR) reported.
"Negotiations are still ongoing," Rosen Zhelyazkov, head of the General Directorate of the Prison Service, told BNR.
It is also expected that members of the Federation of Workers' Trade Unions at the Ministry of the Interior will join the protests.
Why can the universe have more dimensions and we can't see them
Physicists usually don't write bestsellers, but Briana Green of the University of Columbia managed to hit the black in 1999.
His book The Elegant Universe has become the most accessible way for laymen to tap into the thicket of problems that a number of theoretical physicists are dealing with today - an effort to unify two well-functioning but mutually incompatible discoveries of the 20th century: quantum theory and theory of general relativity.
One of the reasons for the success of the Elegant Universe is undoubtedly that in search of a unified physical theory, it is not a boring story.
Physicists have solved the basic problem between the two key theories (for at the moment our universe is de facto divided into two worlds with different laws) and solve it so imaginatively that it is worth retellinging even to lay people.
In addition, Greene handled the complex material so freshly and engagingly that it is impossible to blame him for how much interesting he did not include in his book.
Moreover, his "mistake" was mostly corrected in the other two books: The Structure of the Universe and The Hidden Reality.
Apparently, even the author wouldn't object if his book obsoleted faster, but the fact is that even after 15 years the Elegant Universe still provides almost everything that an informed layman with the ambition to understand the world should know about this important part of theoretical physics.
It is quite likely that in the coming years it will be a little more vivid in this area (for example, due to the fact that the LHC accelerator will finally go to "full throttle"), and so it is a good time to enter the Greens non-violently open door to the world of modern physics.
How much size can our universe have?
The idea that our universe may have more than three spatial dimensions may sound foolish, fantastic, strange or mystical.
However, it is specific and completely acceptable.
In order to understand this, let’s look away from the universe as a whole for a moment to something more grounded, namely a long and thin garden hose for watering.
Imagine stretching the hundred-meter garden hose from one side of the canyon to the other and tracking the whole scenery from a half-kilometre distance (as in the picture and on the following page).
From such a distance, you can easily notice a long hose in a horizontal direction, but if you do not just suffer from sharpness, the thickness of the hose can hardly be recognized.
Due to its great distance from the hose, you would think that an ant forced to live on a hose has only one dimension in which it can walk: the left-right dimension along the hose.
When someone asks you where the ant was at any given time, you answer it with just one indication: the distance of the ant from the left (or right) end of the hose.
All we have to say is that from a half-kilometre distance, a long hose looks like a one-dimensional object.
In the real world, the hose has a thickness.
If we enlarge everything, we will suddenly see a second dimension - in the shape of a circle wrapping the hose.
In such an enlarged view, it is obvious that the ant can actually move in two independent dimensions: in the already well-known left-right dimension along the length of the hose, but also in the "dimension in/against the direction of the movement of the clock hands", i.e. around the circular cross-section of the hose.
You begin to understand that to determine the position of a small ant, you have to enter two numbers: how far from the end of the hose and where there is a hose on the circle.
This reflects the fact that the surface of the hose is two-dimensional (Experts will notice that this chapter concentrates purely on the fault theory of strings.
It discusses non-miscoring aspects Chapters 12 and 13.).
The surface of the hose is two-dimensional: one long longitudinal dimension is illustrated by a straight arrow, the dimension in the direction of the perimeter, marked with a circular arrow, is short and coiled.
However, there is a clear difference between these two dimensions.
The dimension along the hose is long and easily visible.
The size of the wriggling hose circumference is short, "coiled" and harder to notice.
In order to realize the existence of a circular dimension, we had to examine the hose with significantly better resolution.
This example illustrates an important feature of spatial dimensions.
That they are divided into two groups.
They can be either large, large, and therefore directly noticeable, or, on the contrary, small, coiled and much worse observable.
Of course, in this case, we did not exactly pretend to reveal the "coiled" dimension wrapping the thickness of the hose.
All you had to do is take a telescope.
But if the hose had been thinner - like a hair or a capillary - we would have uncovered a coiled dimension only with great effort.
Kaluza sent Einstein his article in 1919, in which he came forward with the overwhelming idea that the spatial geometry of the universe could have more than three dimensions known to all of us.
Kaluza justified his radical assertion by saying that the additional dimension provides an elegant and compelling framework in which Einstein's general relativity and Maxwell's electromagnetic theory can be woven into a single, unified conceptual structure.
Immediately, the question arises as to how Kaluza's observation goes together with the obvious fact that we are seeing just the three dimensions of space.
The answer, which Kaluza quietly assumed between the lines and which was clearly pronounced and clarified by Swedish mathematician Oskar Klein in 1926, stands and falls, claiming that the spatial geometry of our universe can have both large and coiled dimensions.
This means that, like the dimension in the direction of the length of the hose, our universe has large, vast and easily visible three dimensions, the existence of which we become aware of at any moment.
But by analogy with the circular perimeter of the garden hose, the universe can also have additional dimensions, firmly rolled into space so small that they have so far remained hidden even from our most perfect experimental apparatus.
In order to get a clearer picture of the essence of Kaluza's remarkable design, let's stay with the hose for a little while longer.
Imagine drawing a relatively dense series of circles on the perimeter of the hose in black.
From a distance, the hose still looks like a thin one-dimensional line.
Thanks to the drawing, we will reveal the coiled dimension even more easily, because we will see a motif from Figure 2.
We can clearly see that the hose surface is two-dimensional, with one dimension large and almost unlimited, and with the other short and circular.
Kaluza and Klein came up with the idea that our universe has a similar structure, but apart from one small circular dimension, it has three large spatial dimensions, together four-space dimensions.
It is difficult to draw an object with too many dimensions.
In order to help our imagination a little, let's notice the illustration in Fig.3; it shows two large dimensions and one small circular dimension.
In the picture we increase the view of the geometry of the space similarly as we enlarged the surface of the hose.
Each subsequent level, represents a huge magnification of the geometry of space from the previous level.
Our universe may have additional dimensions (we can see them at the fourth level of magnification) if they are coiled into a sufficiently small space; this explains to ourselves that we have not yet directly observed them.
The square network depicts commonly known "large" dimensions, while the circle represents a new, tiny and coiled dimension.
Just as thread loops in a densely woven carpet, even these circles exist in every place of the usual dimensions - we only drew them, for the sake of illustrativeness, only into intersections in a square mesh.
The background of Figure 4 illustrates the commonly known structure of space - the ordinary world around us - in such common scales as meters,represented by the sides of a small square in a square network.
In each picture below, we will focus on the small area of the previous image; we will enlarge it so that it becomes visible.
Initially nothing special happens, as we can see on the first few levels of magnification.
However, when we progress on our way to the microscopic properties of the geometry of space further - to the fourth level of magnification in Figure 3 - we suddenly see a new, coiled dimension in the shape of a circle, similar to the loops of thread in a densely woven piece of carpet.
Kaluza and Klein came up with the idea that an additional circular dimension exists in every place in the direction of large dimensions, just like the hose has a circular circumference at every point of its length.
For the sake of clarity, we have drawn a circular dimension only in some, regularly spaced points.
Figure 4 summarizes Kaluza and Klein's idea of the microscopic structure of space geometry.
The similarity with the hose is obvious, even though we notice important differences.
First of all, the universe has three large, far-living spatial dimensions (of which we have drawn only two), while the hose has a large size of only one.
An even more important difference is that we are now talking about the spatial geometry of the universe itself, not just some object inside the universe, like our hose.
But the basic idea is the same.
If the additional circular dimension is extremely miniature, recognising it - like the circular perimeter of the hose - is much heavier than observing the obvious, large and vast dimensions.
If the size of the additional dimension is small enough, it will actually reveal it beyond the strength of our state-of-the-art magnification tools.
But the most important thing is that an additional dimension is not just a round bump within ordinary dimensions, as the two-dimensional illustration mistakenly suggests.
The circular dimension is a new dimension that exists at every point of three common large dimensions.
It's a dimension to the remaining three dimensions independent just as the dimensions are from above-to-down, from left-to-right and front-to-back independent (and perpendicular) to each other.
Sufficientely a small ant could move in all four dimensions, and to determine its position we would need four data, in addition to the three usual still position in the circular dimension; if we also calculate the time, then the data five, in any case one more than we would normally expect.
Have You Been Intrigued?
You can buy Brian Greeen's Elegant Universe book here at books.idnes.cz.
The price for the e-book is 149,- CZK.
To our surprise, we find that even if we are aware of the existence of only three dimensions of space, Kaluza’s and Klein’s reasoning shows that the existence of additional coiled dimensions is not excluded, if they are small enough.
The universe may have more dimensions than we can see with the naked eye.
How small are they supposed to be?
State-of-the-art technical equipment can recognize the structures of a large billionth of a billionth of a meter.
We can barely observe the smaller coiled dimensions.
In 1926, Klein combined Kaluza's original idea with a few ideas from just-navigating quantum mechanics.
His calculations suggested that an additional circular dimension could have a size of approximately one Planck length, that is, a size far beyond the distinctiveness of today's instruments.
Since then, physicists have referred to the possibility of an additional small spatial dimensions Kaluza-Klein's theory.
