She walked on the wings
Aviation day in Cheb.
The Boeing Stearman PT-17 machine featured aviation acrobat Peggy Walentin, who climbed from the cockpit to the bar on the upper wing of the biplane and also between the wings.
There it is dependent only on the strength of the muscles.
The air day in Cheb took place for the fifth time this year and the organizers again managed to prepare some interesting aerial pieces.
It premiered at the oldest Czech airport not only "walking on wings" performed by German acrobat Peggy Walentin, but also the legendary P-51 D Mustang fighter or Beechcraft 18.
He drew attention to himself with his radiant cover.
Acrobatics called Wingwalking originated in the United States.
After World War 1, many former war pilots tried to assert themselves.
However, due to a large number of fatal accidents, it was banned.
Today, however, it is slowly returning to the schedule of flight days.
Peggy climbs out of the cockpit to the upper wing after the start.
That's where he performs various exercises.
At the end of the performance she will move into the space between the wings of the aircraft, where her show continues," described Luděk Matějíček from the Ultralight Club in Cheb and the organizer of the air day.
The pilot of the Boeing Stearman 75 E is her husband Friedrich.
If Peggy's performance was the highlight of the program, then the subsequent performance of the Mustang fighter was the famous icing on the cake.
The legendary fighter rushed to the surface with the Grippens of the Army of the Czech Republic (so-called in numbers).
Such line-up could be seen by aviation fans in the Czech Republic for the first time.
Pilot Miroslav Sázavský then showed everyone breathtaking acrobatics with an aircraft from 1945.
It was still made during the war, but probably never participated.
He's been included in military reserves.
From 1963 he worked in the civilian sphere.
Even as a racing aircraft.
Today he has his home hangar at the airport in Munich Hradiště.
In the same place is also another pearl of the Cheb air day, the twin-engine Beechcraft C45H Expeditor.
There was not a massive machine overlooked at the airport.
His polished aluminium cover shone into the distance.
The Max Holste MH 1521M Broussard has also been admired in the colours of the French Air Force.
A small airliner jumped in for a renounced Turbolet and brought part of the German delegation from Hofa, Germany, for an aerial day.
In Cheb, the German neighbors presented themselves with the reconstructed aircraft Jak 18T or the three-member biplane formation Bücker Jungmann.
