Genetic disorders are often misdiagnosed
A woman from Britain describes how she thought she would die for many years after a misdiagnosis.
After being diagnosed with another disease, Karin Rodgers spent most of her adolescence thinking she had only a few years to live.
In fact, it was Charcot-Marie-Tootho syndrome (CMT) - a group of hereditary disorders that damage nerves outside the brain and spine.
Charity organisation CMT U.K. said a misdiagnosis is a common problem among people with CMT because very little is known about the disease.
Around 23,000 people are believed to be suffering from CMT in the United Kingdom, which may show symptoms in the field of motor skills such as muscle weakness, clumsy gaiting and twisted toes.
Numbness or pain can also occur in those affected, this condition is incurable and progressive, which means that over time, the symptoms worsen.
When Rodgers was thirteen, she thought she was suffering from Freidreich ataxia (FA) - a disease that has a very poor prognosis.
She believed that before she celebrated her 18th birthday, she'd be confined to a wheelchair, and dead until she was thirty.
The mother of two, who is now 51, says: "As a child, I knew I couldn't do the same things as others.
Every day I fell and everything took me longer.
“I’ve never been able to ride roller skates or skateboard with other kids, and I’ve been bullied at school because of the way I’ve walked and run.”
When Rodgers was thirteen and after several surgeries that were supposed to release her Achilles tendons and straighten her feet, she took advantage of the fact that her doctor had left the room, and looked into her documentation, where she learned she was suffering from the FA.
“I felt guilty because I was snooping, so I didn’t tell anyone, but when I came home, I looked up information about the illness in the library and wrote to the FA Association,” she says.
When they answered, I was shocked, I completely panicked.
I thought I'll end up in a wheelchair before I'm eighteen, and before I'm twenty-five, I'll be dead, and in the meantime I'll gradually lose all my powers.
I was all alone and I was planning a funeral.”
When she was seventeen, she realized that her ability to walk had not deteriorated as much as she had anticipated, and asked her surgeon about it.
He just stood up, hugged her and said, “Honey, I don’t think you have the disease, otherwise you’ll be in a wheelchair.
I think it's going to be something much less life-threatening.'
After undergoing several genetic tests, it was found that he had CMT.
“When he explained to me what CMT is, I thought I was a child of fortune,” she said.
The charity CMT UK has launched a Month of CMT Awareness to draw attention to the disease.
Entrepreneurs in the Washington area: with the turnover of the economy rising taxes
The growing economy means more customers, higher sales and new jobs.
And higher taxes, too.
According to a new survey released last week, state and local taxation of firms in the country with accelerating recovery of the economy grew slightly last year and firms in Washington were no exception.
Entrepreneurs in Maryland and Virginia, combined, paid $27.6 billion to state and local coffers for fiscal and local coffers, 3.8 percent higher than $26.6 billion collected in 2012.
The tax burden - both state and local - rose 4.3% last year to $671bn compared to 3.9% last year, marking its third consecutive growth year after 2009 and 2010, when taxes were falling.
According to a study conducted by professional firm Ernst & Young and the political group Center on State Taxation, state taxes increased faster, by 4.3% than local levies, by 3.9%.
More than halve the tax revenue of the precinct, 56%, is from a trading tax, and 36% of Maryland's income is from companies.
Virginia, with 28 percent, reports in proportion to the smallest tax revenue from the business.
The researchers say that the majority share of tax revenue growth comes from an increase in the value of real estate belonging to firms, which increased property taxes this year this year after three consecutive years, with growth of -1 percent increased by 3.7%.
Although most of the profits come from major states like California, New York, and Texas, the situation appears to be the same in Washington.
Companies in D.C., Maryland and Vriginia last year paid over ten billion dollars in both state and local property taxes, representing an annual increase of 4.2% compared to 9.6 billion in 2012.
However, the apparent increase in the value of real estate is not so much driven up by state and local tax revenues in Maryland, where property tax accounts for less than a fifth of corporate taxation.
For companies in Virginia and D.C., property tax accounts for nearly half state and local taxation.
“In Maryland, he owns a lot of companies’ properties, especially the tax base around D.C., the government, or non-profit organizations that don’t pay taxes,” explained Douglas Lindholm, executive director of the Center on State Taxation.
So Maryland is forced to rely much more, for example, on income taxes to pay for the same services that are in other states.
A recent increase in business tax revenues cannot be credited only to the healing real estate market.
According to the data, business revenues also appear to be increasing.
Firms in the region have published a $2.3 billion in income tax on commercial companies, which have risen from £2.1 billion from 2012.
Maryland has collected one billion dollars in corporate income taxes, the most in the region.
Although they are moving in the same direction, the local tax burden on entrepreneurs does not grow just as quickly in these three places.
The growth rate in D.C. was at the national average, at 4.3%, while Maryland's growth was significantly higher, 4.9%.
In Virginia alone, tax increases were below the average, to 4.1%.
Of all three districts (all of which have the same taxation), Virginia has the lowest corporate income taxation, 6%.
Maryland's corporate tax rate is 8.25%, while the D.C. average remains relatively high, at 9.975 percent.
A similar study carried out by the U.S. Chamber of Commerce this year has shown that Virginia has lower state and local taxation and an overall more favorable tax climate than Maryland.
D.C. did not evaluate the study.
Despite this advantage, Virginia is facing pressure from competition from the South.
North Carolina recently signed into law reducing corporate tax from 6.9% to 6%, as did Virginia, and next year the tax will be reduced to 5%.
If the state continues to meet income targets in the coming year, the statutory rate of income taxation could be reduced to 3% in 2017.
According to a recent study, small entrepreneurs in Washington are doing better, and that's whater higher state and local taxes.
A trade tax levied by small entrepreneurs as a personal income tax - a structure to offset an increase in costs by increasing prices, according to which most firms are being organised - in D.C., Maryland and Virginia rose 20% last year to $2.4 billion, much faster than other taxes.
The researchers say that the trend and the nationwide increase in the trade tax are likely to continue next year, the total tax revenue from sales for state and local governments will increase to 6.2% compared to the first three quarters of 2014.
Washington, however, may fall behind others, as cutting state spending is taking its toll on the labor market.
