The second fundamental lie of creationism is the belief that the sacred texts were written by God and not by real human authors.
When believers debate one of the many things that contradicts their religion, they often put us before the question: Who will you believe?
The supposed word of God, or the word of man?
As if human inquiry were quite insignificant in comparison with the authority they imagine to have their doctrine.
However, when they say "man," they talk about science.
And when they refer to "the word of God," they refer to myths about God enrolled by people.
If there really is an intelligent with a creator with a purpose, then it is he who has created a fossil record that reveals evolutionary history, and it must be he who has devised the genetic patterns that correspond to it, and it must be he who has supplied another series of evidence that points exclusively to the evolutionary conclusion to the last detail.
Why else would all of these things exist?
Seems like he's trying to tell us something!
People couldn't create any of those things.
But people can tell stories, fabulate.
And it was the people who wrote all those lyrics pretending to be a word of God.
Every supposedly holy teaching of any religion in the world describes itself as written by men, not gods.
People who were "moved" or "inspired" by their favorite gods, or perhaps listened to the dictates of angels, but those lyrics were not written by anyone other than mere mortals with a certain degree of imagination, not angels, and certainly not gods.
If there really was one real god, it would be an integral part of all the gods of all religions, an über-galactic supergenius, the ultimate entity of the entire cosmos.
If a being of this size had ever written a book, there would have been only one such book, the only scripture.
It would be known everywhere in the world, without predecessors, parallels or alternatives in any language, because only human authors would not be able to compare with such a work.
And people wouldn't have to trust her blindly, because she'd be consistent, backed by verifiable and truthful evidence, and she'd reveal deep morality and wisdom far beyond all human limits.
It would constantly inspire every reader to the unity of a common faith.
If God had written it, we wouldn't have expected anything less.
But what we're seeing instead is the exact opposite.
Instead of a single religion leading to the final truth, we have many different religions without a common origin, and all of them are steadily dissipating into increasingly separate denominations, seeking contentious truths, and each in some way claim God’s guidance, regardless of the continuing unraveling and separation in every way.
The Jewish Torah, the Christian Gospels, the Islamic Qur'an, Bahá'u'llah's Kitab-i-Akdas, Zarathushtra's Avesta, Sikh Adi-Granth, Mahabharati Bhagavad-Gita, the Book of Mormon, or the Book of Urantius are all considered to be "absolute truth" and "revealed words of the only true god."
The only logical explanation probably is that to some extent, they are all astray.
None of the books mentioned above has any advantage over others.
None of them have evidence, and none can be confirmed historically.
My name is Dr. Paul Maier, and I'm a professor of ancient history at Western Michigan University.
I'm not saying I've proved that the Bible is reliable and historically accurate.
Of course it isn't.
You still have to have faith.
All of them require faith and also apologies (advocacies) because they contain inconsistencies, absurdities, and primitive notions once considered true, which have already been overcome.
So it cannot be said that they are evidence of divine wisdom.
Many of them promote heinous atrocities instead of morality, and many of them are said to have been confirmed by the fulfillment of prophecies - with the fact that each may also contain prophecies that have not been fulfilled.
Leading theologians admit that all sacred texts of any religion have been written with human hands and are therefore the subject of interpretations, impressions and perspectives of their primitive and often prejudicies tied by politically motivated authors, thus explaining many of the contradictions and inconsistencies in these books, especially those in the Bible.
I wouldn't call it a contradiction, more like mutual comments.
We have to point out once again that we have two different authors whose work has somehow been mixed together during some editorial review.
He's right that Genesis has more than one author, and it's certainly not an eyewitness!
Some experts now distinguish four different sources only for Pentateuch, i.e. the five Books of "Moses", and Moses are not attributed the authorship of any of them, because it almost certainly didn't exist as described in the Bible.
Experts agree that Genesis was composed (probably by Ezdrash) of several mutually unrelated oral traditions less than 2500 years ago.
Other documents have been filtered at the same time and are all attributed to human authors.
The Bible is not as old as its believers think.
Dead Sea Scrolls are the oldest archaeological findings of texts known for their unfinished foundation of all Western monotheistic beliefs, and yet they are many centuries younger than the roots of Zoroastrianism, Buddhism, Hinduism, Hellenism, Druidic cults, Chinese or Egyptian religion.
Christianity began as a Gnostic faith, followed by docts and ebionites, and their completely different views of Jesus, which were eventually combined into a kind of compromise called orthodoxy.
Other Christian groups like the Luciferians were dispersed and discredited by further revisions to the Bible.
One of these modifications relates to King Ugaritus, who ruled 3500 years ago.
Because his followers posed a major threat to the young Moses religion, the authors of the New Testament decided to ridicule and Ba'al Zebula, "Lorder on the mountain" was changed to Belzebuba "The Lord of the Flies."
So the Bible was deliberately and fraudulently altered for both religious and political reasons.
The remaining part of what became the New Testament was canonized in the 4th century A.D
Four Gospels were received and sixteen others were dismissed by simply raising their hands, as if the reality of a thing could be accepted or rejected by vote.
