The participant of the bucket challenge dislocated her jaw
As she poured icy water on her head, Isabelle Roberts of the United Kingdom screamed by damaging her facial bone structure.
This bucket challenge didn't work.
The woman was hospitalized after shouting during a bucket challenge by dislocating her jaw.
While she poured icy water on her head, Isabelle Roberts screamed so intensely that she damaged the structure of the bones in her face.
“The water was so cold that I had to scream, but my jaw began to stiffen,” she told The Mirror.
A twenty-year-old girl pours water on herself.
Isabelle Roberts moments before the accident
I tried to close my mouth, but I couldn't, it was stuck, and I realized something was wrong.
Then we got it, and my sister, they could've laughed, but I had to go to the emergency room quickly.'
The 20-year-old girl had to be hospitalized to return her jaw to its original location after taking part in a fashionable viral wave on Tuesday.
Thousands of people shared it on Facebook and Twitter.
An attacker at a Chinese school stabbed nine people, three of whom died
According to New China, a man named Chen forcibly invaded the elementary school building in the morning and attacked around half past eleven local time.
Three of the nine people he stabbed later died in hospital.
Chen later killed himself by jumping from above, New China said, citing the regional government.
In recent years, China has experienced a series of similar attacks in schools and against children.
Therefore, the public is calling for greater security measures and better protection of children in a country where many married couples, in accordance with government policy, have only one offspring.
Zeman showed first-graders in Lány how he had to sit at school
The youngest schoolchildren took the ninth graders to their class, where they waited with the teacher for the president to arrive.
He then demonstrated to the children how he had to sit at school himself.
"We had to sit with our hands behind our backs, fortunately you don't expect that anymore, you're already single people," Zeman told the children.
His wife then handed the first-graders a puzzle as a gift.
President Miloš Zeman is in the first class of primary school in Lány.
Education Minister Marcel Chládek started a new school year at a private sports school in Litvínov.
"This year I want to connect Czech education more with Czech sport," one of the reasons why he went to the north of Bohemia on the first day of school.
The school management approached him some time ago at a hockey match in Litvínov.
"They were the first, so I accepted the invitation to participate in the start of the school year," the minister added.
Education Minister Marcel Chládek (ČSSD) started the school year at a private sports school in Litvínov.
115 thousand first-graders entered the first classes this year.
There will be more than 850 thousand children attending primary schools and more than 405 thousand students will be educated in secondary schools, the Ministry of Education estimates.
Nova's new art news director is David Fiala
David Fiala became the new art director of news and journalism at TV Nova since September 1.
He replaces Alan Záruba, who has led the creative department of the whole Nova group since 1 June.
David Fiala previously worked in the graphical department of Z1 news television.
For the last two years under the leadership of Alan Záruba, he participated in the implementation of the complete visual transformation of news sessions Televizní noviny, Střepiny, Prásk! and other publicist programmes of TV Nova.
David Fiala masters very well the synergy of communication between the newsroom, studio management and the graphic department, which is the essence of the work of the art news director.
It has a feeling for typography and pure elegant design, which is a path that we want to take further in the visuals of Nova’s news coverage,” says Alan Záruba, Nova’s creative director.
Apple iCloud reportedly succumbed to hackers, nude images of celebrities made it public
A screenshot with a list of leaked files
So far, an anonymous user on 4chan is posting nude and pornographic images of U.S. celebrities, according to Mashable.com.
It allegedly gained unauthorized access to user accounts on Apple's servers, where disabled users uploaded them by automatically backing up images from iPhones and iPads.
Apple has not yet commented on the issue.
Some celebrities refuse the images as falsified, but have already acknowledged several of those affected.
These include actress Jennifer Lawrence, best known for her title role as Katniss in Hunger Games and Mary Elizabeth Winstead, who starred in Deadly Trap 4.0.
The goal of the hacker is to use the obtained images and videos to enrich.
U.S. magazines have reportedly expressed interest in the footage, but according to reports on 4chan, the hacker prefers money from the community.
According to The Next Web, it’s probably a weakness in iCloud security.
Two days ago, GitHub had a tool that made it possible to obtain passwords for user accounts through brush force, i.e. by automatically repetiting login attempts with different passwords.
This tool has been demonstrably working, but Apple closed the security hole after two days.
What is certain, however, is that potential attackers had plenty of time to gain access to different accounts prior to the publication of this instrument.
iCloud users should, as a precautionary reason, change their password associated with the Apple ID.
What is important is the length of the password, not its complexity.
A length of at least 12 characters is appropriate.
Leaking naked images of celebrities considered a reminder of the safe use of online services.
Although corporate servers such as Apple, Google or Microsoft are secured much better than computers in households or smaller businesses, uploading sensitive photos in unprotected form is an unnecessary risk.
If you want to back up explicit photos or any other sensitive content online, encrypt the files before uploading first.
It is also reasonable to set the automatic backup of photos from your phone so that it takes place only on wi-fi and when connected to the charger.
In this way, you will have the opportunity to move sensitive images "to safety" in time, and most importantly, you will have the space to delete unsuccessful images, so they will not unnecessarily take up space in online storage.
Instructions on how to encrypt files and communication on the Internet, we offer readers HN and IHNED.cz in the form of an e-book by Michal Altair Valášek in PDF format.
A smart way to save for college scripts
Since the price for college scripts for many students exceeds US$1,000 a year, an incoming second-year student came up with a clever way to reduce costs.
He devised a system of borrowing books from classmates from higher-unders, offering nominal compensation, to delay their sale back.
“I’ve gotten a book for calculations, which normally costs 180 dollars, for a semester for ten and for chicken nuggets,” says Schroeder, 19, who studies at Covenant College, Georgia.
The books required in his last semester would cost 430 dollars, he says.
He Spent 120.
According to the university council, the average student spends more than 1,200 dollars a year on textbooks and aids, but students have different options to manage these expenses.
The preferred choice of students becomes online shops and creative solutions like Schroederovo.
Lending textbooks is on the rise, allowing students to use the book for a semester, often at a significant discount than if they had bought it.
Or Inc running more than 250 bookstores on campus sites says book lending has doubled since 2011.
The survey shows that around a quarter of books in university bookstores were borrowed in the last spring semester, according to vice president or Trevor Meyer.
Less than half of the teaching texts are bought, according to the National Association of University Stores in university bookstores.
Here are 101 best ways to succeed.
Shopping Online
The prices of some new books can be up to a third lower on the Internet than in a university bookstore.
The recommended price of the ninth edition of Ron Larson's "Calculus", Bruce Edwards and Robert Hostetler's book is almost 290 dollars, but on the Internet it is possible to buy it on Chegg.com, in a retail specializing in textbooks, a new one for 239.99 dollars.
Building used
If you don't mind other people's remarks or wear and tear, the appropriate option is the teaching texts used.
On Chegg.com, "Calculus" is sold for 93.49 dollars.
Matt Casaday, a 25th senior student at Brigham Young University, says he has paid 42 cent on Amazon.com for a used copy of "Strategic Media Decisions: Understanding The Business End Of The Advertising Business."
The new was sold for 48 dollars.
University teacher Ingrid Bracey, Head of the University of Massachusetts Amherst's College Without Walls proposes that students verify with their teachers whether older editions are acceptable.
Sometimes changes are not relevant to working in seminars.
If so, the older editions can be sold for a fraction of the price of the latest version.
In addition to Chegg and Amazon, eBay Inc's Half.com is also a popular alternative for buying used books.
Borrowing
It is also worth considering borrowing textbooks, if you treat them well, you do not forget to return them and you do not need to have them even after the seminar.
You can save up to 80% on the cost of a new book.
For example, the student could borrow “Calculus” for about twenty dollars for the whole semester.
Get acquainted with the terms of trade in which you borrow the book, including penalties for notes, highlights or wear and tear and damage.
Keep in mind: If you don't return the book in time, the fine can be higher than the price of a new book.
E-books
Another option is to get an e-book instead of a normal book.
Sometimes these books are essentially borrowed in the form of an electronic license valid for a period of time from distributors such as Amazon.com, Chegg and Barnes & Noble Inc.
Chegg lends "Calculus" for six months for approximately $61.
According to Braceyová, students of literary courses can find the best deals, because many classics are now available for free download, while science and technical texts can be very expensive.
In any case, walk around the shops.
Joe Gault, 29, who will soon board Pepperdine Law School, recommends following ISBN because it guarantees you're buying the right book.
Before you order a book over the internet, make sure it’s really in stock, Gault advises.
He convinced himself of it firsthand.
The book he bought was sold out for four weeks, and he had to pay the full price for a book from a university bookstore.
Mud, all mud.
And then they finded torso rifles from the end of the war
The well is located a short walk from St. George's Church.
We uncovered the vegetation around and we began to pull an age-old build-up - about a meter and a half of mud and rotten leaves.
As we got closer to the clay bottom, we hit the barrel," Radomil Novák recounted.
We pulled it out, purified it and discovered that it was a torso of an assault German rifle used by SS troops.
The finding is supposedly unusual.
According to my information, such rifles were produced at the end of the war.
And there were about three hundred thousand of them," Novák added.
All wooden structural items have been decomposed over time, so it is unusable.
Her body, however, is preserved - it holds its shape.
How did he find himself in the well?
There's an explanation.
It may have been dumped there by people who needed to get rid of the weapon used by the SS units, probably out of fear they wouldn't be relegated.
Fascists on the run wouldn't do that, apparently.
They were throwing away weapons in the forest," the finder thinks.
The rifle was the only thing that the enthusiasts of the Duha Jeseníky Movement had found in the well.
"There could have been hand grenades, there used to be a lot of them, that's why we slowed down the excavation work, we carefully felt the mud so that there wouldn't be any misfortune, but there wasn't anything else down there," Novák approached.
The find will end up in the depository of the museum at the chateau in nearby Slezské Rudoltice.
Ukrainian general: I haven't liberated the captured Czech yet
An exclusive interview Aktuálně.cz with Colonel General Volodymyr Ruban, which frees Ukrainian soldiers from the captivity of pro-Russian separatists.
Kiev/Prague - "Excuse me, I have to take it... they're calling me from Donetsko," he apologized eight times during an exclusive interview with Aktuálně.cz, the Ukrainian Colonel General Volodymyr Ruban, otherwise also the director of the local Center for the Liberation of Prisoners of War.
Colonel General Volodymyr Ruban in a televised debate.
At each such interruption, it was not possible not to hear the names of the people, their numbers or the place where they are - or should be - that Ruban specifies with his counterpart at the other end of the phone.
"That'll be it - go there," he concluded one of the lightning discussions with Donetsk, and once again devoted himself to ours.
Volodymyr Ruban is a general in reserve, a former fighter pilot and an active participant in anti-government protests in Maidan.
Even yesterday, when we arranged an interview, he was in Donetsk.
The liberation of Ukrainian soldiers from the captivity of pro-Russian separatists is devoted twenty-four hours a day.
Currently.cz: You may also know that Czechs are on the side of pro-Russian separatists.
Our counterintelligence estimates their numbers up to thirty.
However, there can also be many more.
Have you meted with a Czech prisoner during your negotiations?
Volodymyr Ruban: I have liberated captive Serbs, Dagestans, Chechens, one Swede, Georgians... but not a Czech yet.
But I know who to turn to in Prague if we ran into your captive countrymaker and could rescue him.
Ukrainian soldier captured by separatists
A.cz: What do you advise the Czechs, who join pro-Russian separatists and Ukrainian soldiers capture them?
They should understand that if, as Czechs, they fight in Ukrainian battalions of volunteers and separatists capture them, they will be shot without mercy after questioning, just like any other foreigner.
Separatists consider these men to be hired mercenaries, who, according to them, deserve nothing but death.
However, if, for example, Ukrainian soldiers capture a group of Donetsk separatists - and among them even a Czech - they put him behind bars and treat him like a prisoner of war.
This guy just survives.
The Ukrainian side also behaves like a state in war.
A.cz: And how many Ukrainian prisoners have you managed to save?
If I understand correctly, in exchange.
Some sources say it's already exceeded a hundred men.
Long ago, we've stopped counting how many people we've helped.
But we've just liberated them... they didn't save from war if they decide to continue fighting.
Saviours are at war doctors, surgeons, but we certainly don't.
That's the way it is.
And every day there are more and more.
We're gonna save someone every day.
Sometimes one, sometimes four or five.
And it doesn't always have to be an exchange.
It is sometimes sufficient for the release of captives even a mere human request.
A.cz: It is rumored that in a few months you have gained great authority and even trust with pro-Russian separatists... as well as in Ukrainian society.
How have you managed to build the respect of both warring parties?
Tough question.
One of the officers in Kiev gave me the nickname "crazy general with steel balls."
It doesn't offend me.
And maybe it describes the essence of our work.
As head of Ukraine's Dignity Corps, I've gained some authority before.
I never claim, cheat and always keep my word.
No matter what happens, I act honestly and speak the truth.
Even if it is very unpleasant to the Ukrainian government, or the Donetsk or Luhansk government.
If they torture and kill people?
That's how I simply tell them they're tortured and killed.
And it's crimes.
I often risk my life, I've even been captured in the exchange of prisoners... this work can mean the end to me at any time... I don't have to come back.
A.cz: How many executions and torture of prisoners do you know?
... (long daision)... to torture prisoners, Ukrainians and separatists take refuge in interrogations.
Sadly.
And executions?
These take place only on the Donetsk side.
I have no information about the fact that the Ukrainian army also executes prisoners.
No indication that the Ukrainian military is committing such a thing.
We have an idea about the number of executions, but I can't and I don't want to mention the numbers.
A.cz: Describe to me the characters and motifs of pro-Russian separatists.
There are officers among them, but also veterans from Afghanistan.
They're trying for independence and maybe even democracy in Donetsk or Luhansk - that's what they're saying.
They don't want to play games with Ukrainian oligarchs anymore.
Separatists already have oligarchs full of teeth and are their irreconcilable opponents - for life and death.
As we say - they've eaten to their throats.
By the way, people showed the same dreams and desires on Maidan.
And now I speculate a little: Russia supports separatists, and the West, on the contrary, the Ukrainian state.
I consider one speculation to be great, and the other to be small.
But already this sentence of mine has an irreconcilable contradiction.
A.cz: For example, how does one of the toughest leaders of pro-Russian separatists, Igor Bezler?
Every time I cross the line of the queue, I am unarmed, and I show the soldiers or the militants my bare hands.
And I've always got both sides of those two hundred or three-hundred meters on a fly or in an optical sight.
The faces of the people pointing at me every time I can see well... and they can pull the havoc of their guns at any time.
Paratrooper, experienced colonel with both German and Ukrainian ancestors.
He considers himself a Russian officer, but his family - his mother - lives in Ukraine.
He believes that Ukraine should not exist.
And if so, it's just like a little gubernia in the great Russian empire.
In any case, he constantly talks about it and considers Ukraine to be a kind of misunderstanding.
According to him, such a state should not even exist.
At the same time, it is the irreconcilable enemy of all Ukrainian oligarchs.
A.cz: Speaking of characters - in the discussion show of Ukrainian television, you described your Prime Minister Arsenij Yatsenyuk as a weak, clueless and tired "leader" who would do best if he left quickly.
In those three-hundred-meter walk, I always get at least five times afraid.
So why should I hide the view that Yatsenyuk is a very weak prime minister and that it harms our country?
A.cz: Do you think it has a stake in the war in Ukraine?
You may know that Yatsenyuk's Ukrainians call him a "rabbit."
And the rabbit is not an animal in the eyes of Ukrainians, from which fear would come, or have respect and authority for others.
His behavior directly replicates the behavior of the rabbit.
Maybe he's a good economist, but as prime minister he doesn't have the right to weep in parliament in war, to beg him, to play his intrigues with him, to threaten to take office... he's got to run the country, manage it and protect it in times of war.
If the country is threatened, the prime minister is not entitled to family, friends, rest for weeping emotions.
He is the first minister of Ukraine.
And Yatsenyuk?
She's acting like a teenage girl.
Yes, he's tired, but he's also weak because he's never been strong... he doesn't fight for Ukraine, he's always complaining about everything.
That's why he's supposed to leave.
The sooner, the better for Ukraine.
Why the Guardians of the Galaxy couldn't save sales
