We have to admit it and not try to chase after what's already been done.
In contrast to the current crisis of Hollywood blockbusters, Netflix's profit has reached $1.2bn thanks to internet services, nearly twice as much as last year's $837 million.
Last summer, director Steven Spielberg criticized studios for relying too heavily on comic rights, and predicted Hollywood's "collapse."
In light of the current slump, some commentators declare that there may be something about his catastrophic prophecy.
With the launch of possible big box offices in the summer of 2015 including Avengers: Age of Ultron, Mimoňů and Jurassic World, other numbers are more optimistic in the film industry.
X-Men producer Simon Kinberg recently described the decline in sales as simply “cyclical.” For Hollywood Reporter, he said, “Next summer will be the summer of the biggest blockbusters in history, and no one will worry about sales.”
