Al Pacino has twice thrilled Venice.
The film festival is otherwise experiencing a crisis
After the huge enthusiasm over the opening film Birdman, whose director Alejandro González Iñárritu suddenly became one of the serious candidates for the Golden Lion, the other days indicated the impression that interest in the 71st festival in Venice had somewhat dropped.
Whether because the number of seats in the largest hall has risen to fourteen hundred, or because Birdman was followed by critically acknowledged, but for viewers, less appealing films such as a documentary about genocide in Indonesia in the 60’s The Look of Silence and other social-themed films from America’s 99 homes to Iranian Short Stories, there were simply empty seats in cinemas.
Heroes from comics
Even the red carpet in front of the festival palace has not yet besieged such crowds as in the past, and it has reportedly ceased to be a last-minute problem to find a place in the hotel.
There is talk of a crisis in Italy more than elsewhere, and it is no wonder that it has befalled even esteemed institutions such as Biennale and the world's oldest film festival.
Luckily, the crisis does not manifest itself in the lack of personalities, but present exclusively in connection with films.
While younger fans longed for autographs of comic book superheroes Michael Shannon and Andrew Garfield, who successfully played dramatic roles in 99 homes, for all generations indiscriminately, the main event was a visit to the legendary actor Ala Pacino.
He also starred in two films.
In an out-of-competition-driven story filmed by Oscar-winning Barry Levison based on Philip Roth's novel, seventy-four-year-old Pacino has a role close to him: an actor at the height of his fame who suffers depression from the end of his career and personal life.
But seeing him the very next day as the odd owner of a hardware store from a country town in Manglehorn directed by David Gordon Green was a truly memorable experience.
The Volpi Cup for Best Actors shouldn't miss it - but does an artist of Pacin's format still need prizes?
Perhaps for him the greatest satisfaction is the love of the audience, and of the Italian, in particular, because although he was born in New York, Al Pacino proudly declares himself to the land from which his grandparents come.
Points for Italy
After all, the Italian film is doing very well in Venice this year.
Directed by Francesco Munzi, black souls became Birdman's first film rewarded with a long applause.
The Calabrian offshoot of the mafia is overlooked by the view of family relationships between clans that fight each other for power and money, and the director was able to reconcile all the prerequisites for a good film: a quality screenplay, camera, music and actors.
He was also impressed by chamber psychothriller Hungry Hearts, who filmed Saverio Costanzo with Alba Rohrwacher in the role of a woman who, with her misguided anxiety, almost destroys her own child.
Conversely, the French competition films did not receive a warm reception either ransom for fame or 3 hearts, both works clinging to the traditional model of consumer films, which are based on the popularity of actors.
But after all, two really excellent films, Birdman and the Black Soul, are not so little for the first half of the festival.
And even though the festival's films have mostly depressive themes, Peter Bogdanovich's new film called She's Funny That Way, a funny comedy released in Venice outside the competition and filmed in the style of the American merries of the 40's, managed to lift the mood for more than one day.
Police caught four meth producers in Prague, drugs driven from Poland
"In recent days, detectives have arrested three men between the ages of 42, 42 and 37 and a twenty-five-year-old woman who, from last November until this August, produced and sold meth drugs in Prague 9," police spokeswoman Jana Rösslerová said.
First, on Tuesday, August 12, the intervention unit in Cihlářská Street caught a forty-two-year-old organizer and dealer in one person and a young woman, who figured in the group as drug purchasers in Poland and a batch seller.
The following day, detectives arrested, based on the prior consent of the prosecutor of another member of the group, a thirty-seven-year-old man.
The last of the fource officers were caught on Wednesday morning on August 20 on the premises of the Malešice incinerator," the spokeswoman described.
For transporting drugs from Poland, the woman received a third of the drug
The main organizer had a wide field of activity.
In the workshop of the Letná complex, in the garden house in Horoměřice, in an apartment in Koněvova street and in other places in Prague 9 he produced meth, provided various aids and chemicals necessary for its production and provided transport of drugs with pseudoephedrine from the Republic of Poland.
He further weighed the manufactured drug, divided it into plastic bags and subsequently sold meth.
Twenty-five-year-old, an unpunished woman assisted him with transporting drugs from Poland or selling drug addicts.
For this, she received a reward from the organizer in the form of a third of the manufactured drug," Rösslerová added.
Criminals also carried out several house searches based on the judge's order, where they secured the drug, industrial chemicals, digital scales, salary bags, syringes, tubes and other aids and items, which, according to the experts, can be considered as a complete equipment for the production of methamphetamine.
The dealer has been punished seven times since 1995
"From the available records, the officers found out that the main organizer had been sentenced seven times since 1995 for property crime, the woman was not punished in court," the police spokeswoman added.
The last detainee was conditionally sentenced in 2006 for the offence of sexual abuse, and the thirty-seven-year-old car mechanic, who is prosecuted at large, has been sentenced nine times since 2009.
For him, it was mostly for property crime.
The investigator accused three men and a woman of committing the offence of illicit manufacturing and other treatment of narcotics and psychotropic substances and poisons, for which they face up to ten years in prison.
In the end, the investigator eventually filed a complaint with the public prosecutor on the application for detention, where the judge eventually placed them," the spokeswoman said.
The fourth accused, a thirty-seven-year-old recidivist, ended up at large.
His position and role in this case was not as fundamental as with the other defendants.
Police continue to work with this man.
