Valls calls on the heavily fractured left to "show its support" for a combatively tuned socialist president whose new division of functions has not been met with the consent of the majority of the French.
The president deserves universal respect, he deserves loyalty, he deserves our support.
"It's our duty to stay by his side," he said, reaping applause.
As a conciliatory gesture, the prime minister insisted that his government would not question France's controversial thirty-five-hour working week, despite passionate conjecture by Emmanuel Macron, the new finance minister, who earlier this week suggested that the rules be relaxed.
On Saturday, Hollande demanded socialists to remain "cohesive" with the government.
Christiane Taubir, the justice minister, took a line over his budget when she appeared at a meeting of rebellious Socialist MPs, where she criticized the Socialist Party for "losing faith in the future because of it."
