"And it won't last until the end of autumn or winter," Le Pen told her supporters.
She reiterated her party's call to dissolve Parliament, saying it was convinced that it could win early elections for the party and that it was ready to run the country.
France's ruling party suffered a defeat in the March municipal elections, and in May's extremely Conservative right-wing succeeded in elections to the European Parliament.
According to the Journal du Dimanche poll, 76 percent of French people think that the Socialist Party is at the risk of being divided into several competing factions before the end of Hollande’s tenure in 2017.
Pascal Perrineau, political scientist at the University of Sciences Po, has warned that the French will soon run out of patience if the new socialist government fails to improve the economy and reduce record unemployment.
"It's limited space for opportunity, but public opinion needs to think there are changes in the situation," he warned.
Otherwise, the situation could get worse.
