Last week he placed a victory for the proponents of Scottish independence at his fingertips
The Scotch independence campaign was in danger of making that mistake for the third time, when it nearly placed a question on billboards all over Scotland whether the Scots wanted to be rich.
But you don't get to the hearts of Scottish voters by appealing to their wallet, you won't even earn their respect if you start threatening them.
The morality of the New Testament is still alive in Scotland, even though few Scots still go to church today.
Scots have a far greater tendency to expel billorders from the temple than let them dictate how to vote.
Scotch nationalist leader Alex Salmond knows this well, and that's whay, in a televised discussion, he repeatedly asked his rival, Unionist Darling, whether he was willing to respect the "sovereign will of the Scottish people" if Scottish voters voted for independence, and to start supporting the single currency for both Scotland and England.
This forced Darling to vote between Scottish civil society and London financiers, and with a grimace he fell into that trap.
Of course Darling doesn't support the mandate of Scottish voters.
However, the discussion of what kind of currency Scotlia will have has already been more or less neutralised.
What really motivates Scottish voters is the fairness, job opportunities and defence of the public health service that citizens have for free.
Darling backed London bankers on the currency and also gave the impression that he would not protest against the privatization of health care.
By the way Salmond put it, "he climbed into bed with the Conservatives."
That's a very dangerous position for any Scotch politician.
Every Scotch voter knows Scottish entrepreneurs are in bed with Conservatives.
This was confirmed by David Cameron, who spoke not to voters in Scotland, but to an association of "bosses," to the Confederation of British Industry.
It's becoming clear that Cameron's refusal to debate independence with Scottish Prime Minister Salmond has been a grave mistake for a unionist campaign.
Cameron looks like a cowardly absent-minor domestic.
Scots would respect him if he arrived for a debate with the Scottish Prime Minister.
At least Cameron was able to say that Scotland could be a "successful independent country."
Alistair Darling can't say anything like that, it's causing him almost physical pain.
What he is saying is clear: Scots are not supposed to pop up, and they have to accept what it is.
