It's just from the idea that his stomach's clenching, and his head's starting to hurt.
A head that is even harder to hold a mathematics lesson, that evil game for the chosen ones, among whom I don't belong and will never be.
Again, those threes and fours, God forbid fives, hide from rehearsals, fit into the benches and best sublimate, be there and not be there, to exist only as a name in the classroom and to dream stories about nicer girls and nicer buildings, about antennas to the universe, where no one competes with anyone, humiliates no one, and does no leave.
He's had the longest year for ten months.
And it's only the first day, now like years ago.
She let her daughters die.
She is threatened with exceptional punishment - Novinky.cz
The first birth took place in March 2012 in the cabin of public toilets at the bus station in Jihlava.
Without foreign help, a little girl was born, which she wrapped in a prepared blanket after cutting the umbilical cord, hid under a jacket and carried it to the road feeder, where she put it in bushes located several dozen meters from the road.
She gave birth on her way to school
It probably came to me a little earlier.
I've been on my way to school.
On the bus I needed to go to the bathroom, that's where I gave birth.
She was born alive, looking at me.
I cut the umbilical cord with scissors, which I wore to school," the defendant described, stating that she was embarrassed about the pregnancy.
"On the following day she returned to the place, put the baby's body in a backpack and took it away, removed the body from his backpack, wrapped it in a black plastic bag and hid it and left under the staircase in her grandmother's house in Havlíčkův Brod," the lawsuit reads, adding that it is not entirely obvious when the newborn's death occurred.
It could have been in tens of minutes, but even a few hours after childbirth, choking when covered with a blanket or when inserted under a jacket, hypothermia or bleeding from an untied umbilical cord.
A similar scenario was repeated next spring.
The woman, at an undetected period from February to April 12, when the baby's corpse was found by accidental witnesses under a bush near a pond in Havlíčkův Brod region, herself secretly gave birth.
This time in the bathroom of an attic apartment in a family house.
Without verifying whether she was born alive without cutting and tying the umbilical cord that was wrapped around her neck, she put the baby in several plastic containers, including a plastic bag, and left it in the bathroom.
Subsequently, in the afternoon, he and his then-partner took him to the pond.
In the end, a friend turned it in
The father of both children was a friend of the defendant, who allegedly did not know about his partner's pregnancy.
Even for this reason, he does not stand trial now, only he was punished for an offense against the burial law.
He, too, was the one who later turned himself in to the police.
I don't like that he isn't punished as complicit.
He manipulated the child against my wishes.
I know my solution wasn't right, but it felt like human at that moment," the defendant, who allegedly succumbed to the man's relationship and was afraid of him, didn't hide the outrage.
In the course of her testimony, the young woman spoke fairly evenly.
Tears drove into her eyes only the moment when she spoke about her relationship with her parents, from whom she left after reaching the age of majority.
She didn't want to kill
"They visit me every 14 days, they do everything for me, even though I know I don't deserve it," she said.
I didn't buy equipment because I didn't want children, I felt young.
I kept wanting to put him in a baby box, but I don't know what happened that I didn't.
I can't explain that to you," she replied to the judge's question.
However, she declined to act deliberately and intent to kill the newborn.
According to her claim, the second child was born dead.
However, she did not stop drinking alcohol during her pregnancy.
However, I limited it.
She didn't drink hard.
“I’ve also restricted smoking,” she said.
Although her pregnancy could be seen in both cases, she told everyone that she was having health problems, that she was irrigated.
Even a friend.
The main trial will continue with the testimony of forensic experts at the Hradec Králové court on Tuesday.
A verdict could have already been handed down by Wednesday.
The woman is threatened with a penalty of up to 18 years in prison, possibly an exceptional sentence.
The Norwegian Cruise is approaching a three-billion-dollar repurchase of Prestige Cruise
According to sources familiar with the whole affair, Norwegian Cruise Line Holdings s.r.o. (NCHL.O), the world's third-largest cruise operator, has progressed in negotiating the purchase of Prestige Cruises International Inc. for approximately three billion dollars.
The trade by Norwegian Cruise, a $6.8-billion-dollar market company that competes with greater competition with Royal Carrebian Cruises s.r.o. (RCL.N) and Carnival Corp (CCL.N), allowed access to Prestige Cruises luxury cruise ships and wealthy clients.
The deal could be released as early as this week, the source revealed on Sunday, but cautioned that negotiations may still not end successfully.
Prestige Cruises, a private investment company Apollo Global Management LLC (APO.N), also owns a twenty percent stake in Norwegian Cruise.
Sources did not wish to be named because the negotiations are not public.
Norwegian Cruise and Prestige Cruises officials did not respond to the request for comment, an Apollo spokesperson declined to commente.
Located in Miami, Norwegian Cruise operates thirteen cruising cruise ships in North America, the Mediterranean and the Baltic Sea, Central America and the Caribbean.
The company's revenue in 2013 was US$2.57 billion, 13% more than in 2012.
Prestige Cruises operates under Oceania and Regent brands, which together own eight cruise ships sailing to Scandinavia, Russia, the Mediterranean, North America, Asia, Africa and South America.
In 2013, the company reported revenue of US$1.2 billion, 6% more than in the previous year.
A twenty-nine-billion-dollar cruise segment is expected to capitalize in the coming year from the rise of the middle-class in growing economies such as China and India.
The companies compete to become the preferred option for these customers when choosing cruises.
Prestige Cruises registered its first public offer with U.S. regulators in January 2014.
Apollo has had a majority stake in the company since 2007 since the close of the deal worth US$850 million.
Norwegian Cruise has existed in its current form since 2000, when it merged with the cruise operator owned by Genting Bhd (GENT.KL), a consortium of leisure businesses and casinos controlled by Malaysian billionaire Lim Kok Thay.
In 2008, Apollo invested one billion dollars in Norwegian Cruise.
Norwegian Cruise listed the shares on the stock exchange in January 2013.
According to regulatory authorities at the end of June, Genting owned an equity stake of 28%, Apollo 20% and private investment company TPG Capital LP 8%.
According to the registration dossier, the first public offering includes Carnival, Royal Caribbean Cruises and Norwegian Cruise combined 82% of the volume of North American passengers.
Hongong pro-democracy activists provoke Chinese authorities a day after voting decision
There was a group of people loyal to Beijing who were waving the Chinese flag.
On Sunday, the Permanent Commission of the National People's Congress approved that only two out of three candidates should take part in the 2017 elections in Hong Kong.
All candidates must first win the support of an appointment committee, which is likely to consist of members loyal to Beijing.
Because of this decision, it is almost impossible for opposition Democrats to get to the ballotages and get pro-democratic activists to renew their vows and cripple Hong Kong's financial center with protests of the "Occupation of the Headquarters" movement.
In Hong Kong, political reform is the main reason for tensions, Chinese leaders fear the demand for democratization will spread to other cities.
Following a detailed report outlining Chinese official jurisdiction in Hong Kong in June, Democratic activists held an unofficial referendum on elections in a special official district and hundreds of thousands of people marched into the city's business district and occupied it.
The press conference, at which Prime Minister Li will speak, is organized by the government in Hongon and the Chinese liaison office in Hong Kong.
During the day, Vice-Chairman of the Permanent Committee of the Legislative Commission Zhang Ronghsun and the Deputy Director of the State Council of the Hong Kong and Macau Feng Wei Bureau of Affairs were also scheduled to speak at several press conferences.
Studen activists have said they will gather outside the Hong Kong government's office in the afternoon.
Until the last days of colonial rule 150 years ago, Britain had never mentioned democracy in Hong Kong.
Putin asks Kiev to start talks with eastern Ukraine on “state sovereignty”
According to a description of the events by Russian newspapers, Russian President Vladimir Putin on Sunday demanded that the Ukrainian government stop fighting with separatists in the east of the country and immediately begin negotiations on the "state sovereignty" of detached areas.
His spokesman, Dmitry Peskov, later explained that Putin’s remark did not intend to implience that areas of eastern Ukraine under the control of separatists would become part of Russia, but that their status within Ukraine should be reconsidered so that the Russian-speaking region would be given the power to protect its rights and interests.
Putin’s call on the Kiev government to treat pro-Russian rebels as equal corresponds to an obvious strategy, according to which it has been guided since the outbreak of unrest five months ago: Help the separatists occupy the territory and force the Ukrainian government to grant the newly proclaimed Novorussia de facto independence so that instead with the West, it can connect with Russia.
In an interview with the state's first television channel, Putin condemned Ukraine's military actions to regain the eastern regions of Donetsk and Luhansk, which were taken by separatists in March and April after Moscow annexed the Crimean peninsula belonging to Ukraine on March 18.
The Kremlin and separatists of the recently occupied territory referred to as “Novorussia” or “New Russia”, a term that refers to the famous pre-revolutionary days of the Russian Empire.
Russia's Itar-Tass news agency has released Putin's words, according to which "prisoner is an illusion" anyone who believes there are peace talks on the horizon because Ukrainian politicians launched campaigns ahead of parliamentary elections on October 26, while government troops are attacking civilian populations in areas controlled by separatists.
“In the interest of people living in these areas, we must immediately initiate meaningful negotiations, not only on legal matters, but also on the political organization of society and on the status of state sovereignty of the south-east of Ukraine.”
Peskov said Putin’s reference to state sovereignty was meant in the context of broader autonomy, which has been negotiated with the leadership of Kiev for several months and which is intended to alleviate the concerns of Russian-speaking areas from threats to cultural and linguistic rights.
Peskov stated that only the Ukrainian government can grant the necessary autonomy to the eastern regions.
This is not a matter for discussion between Ukraine and Russia, Peskov said, "because this is not a conflict between Russia and Ukraine, but an internal Ukrainian conflict."
The Kremlin spokesperson, which was supposed to corrective a "wrong interpretation" of Putin's remarks, highlighted Russia's approach to resolving separatist insurgencys in the east, which is different from the open occupancy of Crimea, where a majority of the two-million-dollar population belongs to ethnic Russians.
Moscow would have much more trouble annecting Donetsk and Luhansk in eastern Ukraine, as most of the 6.5-million inhabitants are not Russians, and opinion polls that took place before the conflict have shown widespread support for staying in Ukraine.
The autonomy that Russian diplomats discussed in international forums would give regional governments in Ukraine the power to determine their own trade agreements and international relations, essentially giving the Kremlin de facto control over a territory that would connect the Russian mainland with Crimea.
The Black Sea Peninsula annexed five months ago is the headquarters of Russia's main naval fleet, naval trade facilities as well as historic coastal resorts.
Areas between Russia's Rostov and Crimea are also a center of mines, factories and foundries that produce important components for the Russian military.
Speaking during his inaugural speech on June 7, Ukrainian President Petro Poroshenko suggested that Ukrainian lawmakers - after the new elections - consider changes to the constitution that would give diverse regions more control over their finances and language status.
However, this vision of autonomy differs greatly from that of the Kremlin and the separatists. Moscow is accused of arming and inciting violence.
Putin's final call for Kiev to deal with the separatists as equals followed the rebels' further advance last week after Russian troops and tanks from a formerly peaceful area along the Azov Sea penetrated eastern Ukraine.
Separatists reinforced by the Russians have taken control of Novoazovsk with such force that Ukrainian security authorities are concerned that it is a campaign with the aim of occupying strategic coastal territory all the way to Crimea.
This hastened a huge civilian and military effort to fortify Mariupol, a port with metallurgical combinations of 500 000 inhabitants, which lies between Novoazovsk and a narrow gateway to the Crimean peninsula.
Poroshenko spoke at a meeting of European Union leaders in Brussels on Saturday, urging action to prevent further Russian aggression against Ukraine, a former Soviet republic that has been independent for the past 23 years.
"We're approaching a point from which there's no return," Poroshenko warned.
There are now thousands of foreign military units and hundreds of foreign tanks on the territory of Ukraine.
The EU summit took no definitive steps - leaders are calling for further sanctions against Russia in the event of an unspecified escalation of the Ukrainian crisis.
Colonel Andriy Lysenko, a spokesman for Ukraine's National Security and Defense Council, told reporters in Kiev on Saturday that Ukrainian troops had to withdraw from their positions in Ilovaysk after last week two columns of Russian armored vehicles and 1,000 military units penetrated the Donetsk region to support besieged separatists.
The first of the 63 reported Ukrainian soldiers captured in Ilovaysk during Russia's invasion were exchanged on Sunday for 10 Russian paratroopers captured a week ago in the interior of Ukraine, Lysenko said on Sunday.
